{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0111Pym3GjjLcRoptbKe13nr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Evaluate as a top ML venue reviewer (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper introduces Circuit Structure Consensus (CSS), a framework that scores each edge in a transformer computational graph by the fraction of K≥3 circuit discovery methods that independently include it. Full-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) form compact circuits. The paper argues CSS is most valuable as a diagnostic tool: revealing when cross-method agreement is meaningful vs. artifactual.\n\n## Paper Claims\n1. CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3–5× fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45\n2. Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037, n=5)\n3. ACDC's isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this\n4. Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS)\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n- **Circuit discovery**: Cites activation patching [Conmy 2023, Bhaskar 2024], gradient-based [Syed 2023, Marks 2024], representation similarity [Hanna 2024]. Notes distinct inductive biases.\n- **Cross-method comparison**: Cites Mondorf et al. [2025] who compared methods and proposed score averaging within gradient family. Paper differs by using binary consensus and cross-family methods. Also cites PIE [Huo 2024] for iterative pruning ensembles within single paradigm.\n- **Circuit ensembling**: Cites CIRCUS [Parekh 2026] for threshold diversity within single method. Paper claims CSS's cross-method approach succeeds where CIRCUS's within-method approach fails.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n| Task | Model | CSS=1.0 Edges/CPR | Best Single Edges/CPR | CSS Adv. | Max Jaccard |\n|------|-------|-------------------|----------------------|----------|-------------|\n| IOI | GPT-2 | 18/0.112 | 75/0.091 | +0.021 | 0.31 |\n| SVA | GPT-2 | 23/0.941 | 75/0.953 | −0.012 | 0.38 |\n| Arith | GPT-2 | 54/0.919 | 75/0.985 | −0.066 | 0.695 |\n| IOI | Qwen | 19/0.103 | 75/0.091 | +0.012 | 0.55 |\n| Arith | Qwen | 54/0.932 | 75/0.971 | −0.039 | 0.61 |\n\nCSS advantage is positive only on IOI (both models). Negative on Arithmetic. SVA is marginal (−0.012). The Jaccard-utility correlation has n=5 data points.\n\n## Additional Context\n- The paper positions CSS as a **diagnostic framework** rather than claiming it's a superior circuit extraction method\n- The CIRCUS comparison is only on IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation)\n- ACDC binarization pathology is a useful community finding\n- IFR-EP Jaccard=0.55 on Qwen IOI suggests \"different algorithmic family ≠ measurement independence\"\n- The paper covers 3 tasks × 2 models but originally planned more (DocQA abandoned, Gemma-2/Llama not attempted)\n- The paper has 14 references\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the CSS framework? Is it essentially majority voting on binary circuit membership?\n   - Is the \"consensus = intersection\" idea obvious or does the formalization add value?\n   - What is the incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. [2025] who also compared methods?\n   - Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?\n   - Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?\n   - Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**\n   - Are there missing references? Consider: ensemble methods in ML more broadly, voting classifiers, Bayesian model combination, other circuit discovery papers\n   - Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair and accurate?\n   - Should there be discussion of: faithfulness metrics beyond CPR/CMD, other ways to combine circuit outputs (union, weighted), existing diagnostics in mechanistic interpretability?\n   - The paper has only 14 references — is this adequate for the breadth of related work?\n\n3. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**\n   - For each of the 4 claims, assess: Is the evidence sufficient? Is the claim appropriately calibrated?\n   - Is \"diagnostic framework\" framing a strength (honest/modest) or weakness (low ambition)?\n   - Does the paper deliver on its stated contributions?\n\n4. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper well-positioned within the mechanistic interpretability literature?\n   - Does the paper make clear what practitioners should actually do with CSS?\n   - Is the narrative coherent: does the paper tell a clear story from motivation to results?\n   - Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery in practice?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n7. **Score and Confidence**\n   - Overall score (1-6)\n   - Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n   - Brief justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider whether the diagnostic/analytical framing is genuinely useful to the mechanistic interpretability community, even if the method itself is simple.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36795,"cache_read_input_tokens":22928,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":36795},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"fa05d6ad-74e8-48a8-8c3a-f7399b55b5ff"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a32ebe74fd5eaebf4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Evaluate as a top ML venue reviewer (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper introduces Circuit Structure Consensus (CSS), a framework that scores each edge in a transformer computational graph by the fraction of K≥3 circuit discovery methods that independently include it. Full-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) form compact circuits. The paper argues CSS is most valuable as a diagnostic tool: revealing when cross-method agreement is meaningful vs. artifactual.\n\n## Paper Claims\n1. CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3–5× fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45\n2. Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037, n=5)\n3. ACDC's isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this\n4. Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS)\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n- **Circuit discovery**: Cites activation patching [Conmy 2023, Bhaskar 2024], gradient-based [Syed 2023, Marks 2024], representation similarity [Hanna 2024]. Notes distinct inductive biases.\n- **Cross-method comparison**: Cites Mondorf et al. [2025] who compared methods and proposed score averaging within gradient family. Paper differs by using binary consensus and cross-family methods. Also cites PIE [Huo 2024] for iterative pruning ensembles within single paradigm.\n- **Circuit ensembling**: Cites CIRCUS [Parekh 2026] for threshold diversity within single method. Paper claims CSS's cross-method approach succeeds where CIRCUS's within-method approach fails.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n| Task | Model | CSS=1.0 Edges/CPR | Best Single Edges/CPR | CSS Adv. | Max Jaccard |\n|------|-------|-------------------|----------------------|----------|-------------|\n| IOI | GPT-2 | 18/0.112 | 75/0.091 | +0.021 | 0.31 |\n| SVA | GPT-2 | 23/0.941 | 75/0.953 | −0.012 | 0.38 |\n| Arith | GPT-2 | 54/0.919 | 75/0.985 | −0.066 | 0.695 |\n| IOI | Qwen | 19/0.103 | 75/0.091 | +0.012 | 0.55 |\n| Arith | Qwen | 54/0.932 | 75/0.971 | −0.039 | 0.61 |\n\nCSS advantage is positive only on IOI (both models). Negative on Arithmetic. SVA is marginal (−0.012). The Jaccard-utility correlation has n=5 data points.\n\n## Additional Context\n- The paper positions CSS as a **diagnostic framework** rather than claiming it's a superior circuit extraction method\n- The CIRCUS comparison is only on IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation)\n- ACDC binarization pathology is a useful community finding\n- IFR-EP Jaccard=0.55 on Qwen IOI suggests \"different algorithmic family ≠ measurement independence\"\n- The paper covers 3 tasks × 2 models but originally planned more (DocQA abandoned, Gemma-2/Llama not attempted)\n- The paper has 14 references\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the CSS framework? Is it essentially majority voting on binary circuit membership?\n   - Is the \"consensus = intersection\" idea obvious or does the formalization add value?\n   - What is the incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. [2025] who also compared methods?\n   - Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?\n   - Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?\n   - Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**\n   - Are there missing references? Consider: ensemble methods in ML more broadly, voting classifiers, Bayesian model combination, other circuit discovery papers\n   - Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair and accurate?\n   - Should there be discussion of: faithfulness metrics beyond CPR/CMD, other ways to combine circuit outputs (union, weighted), existing diagnostics in mechanistic interpretability?\n   - The paper has only 14 references — is this adequate for the breadth of related work?\n\n3. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**\n   - For each of the 4 claims, assess: Is the evidence sufficient? Is the claim appropriately calibrated?\n   - Is \"diagnostic framework\" framing a strength (honest/modest) or weakness (low ambition)?\n   - Does the paper deliver on its stated contributions?\n\n4. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper well-positioned within the mechanistic interpretability literature?\n   - Does the paper make clear what practitioners should actually do with CSS?\n   - Is the narrative coherent: does the paper tell a clear story from motivation to results?\n   - Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery in practice?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n7. **Score and Confidence**\n   - Overall score (1-6)\n   - Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n   - Brief justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider whether the diagnostic/analytical framing is genuinely useful to the mechanistic interpretability community, even if the method itself is simple.\n","uuid":"cbf96c44-40ad-4939-bf55-2f3a69fcd8e3","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Evaluate as a top ML venue reviewer (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper introduces Circuit Structure Consensus (CSS), a framework that scores each edge in a transformer computational graph by the fraction of K≥3 circuit discovery methods that independently include it. Full-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) form compact circuits. The paper argues CSS is most valuable as a diagnostic tool: revealing when cross-method agreement is meaningful vs. artifactual.\n\n## Paper Claims\n1. CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3–5× fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45\n2. Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037, n=5)\n3. ACDC's isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this\n4. Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS)\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n- **Circuit discovery**: Cites activation patching [Conmy 2023, Bhaskar 2024], gradient-based [Syed 2023, Marks 2024], representation similarity [Hanna 2024]. Notes distinct inductive biases.\n- **Cross-method comparison**: Cites Mondorf et al. [2025] who compared methods and proposed score averaging within gradient family. Paper differs by using binary consensus and cross-family methods. Also cites PIE [Huo 2024] for iterative pruning ensembles within single paradigm.\n- **Circuit ensembling**: Cites CIRCUS [Parekh 2026] for threshold diversity within single method. Paper claims CSS's cross-method approach succeeds where CIRCUS's within-method approach fails.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n| Task | Model | CSS=1.0 Edges/CPR | Best Single Edges/CPR | CSS Adv. | Max Jaccard |\n|------|-------|-------------------|----------------------|----------|-------------|\n| IOI | GPT-2 | 18/0.112 | 75/0.091 | +0.021 | 0.31 |\n| SVA | GPT-2 | 23/0.941 | 75/0.953 | −0.012 | 0.38 |\n| Arith | GPT-2 | 54/0.919 | 75/0.985 | −0.066 | 0.695 |\n| IOI | Qwen | 19/0.103 | 75/0.091 | +0.012 | 0.55 |\n| Arith | Qwen | 54/0.932 | 75/0.971 | −0.039 | 0.61 |\n\nCSS advantage is positive only on IOI (both models). Negative on Arithmetic. SVA is marginal (−0.012). The Jaccard-utility correlation has n=5 data points.\n\n## Additional Context\n- The paper positions CSS as a **diagnostic framework** rather than claiming it's a superior circuit extraction method\n- The CIRCUS comparison is only on IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation)\n- ACDC binarization pathology is a useful community finding\n- IFR-EP Jaccard=0.55 on Qwen IOI suggests \"different algorithmic family ≠ measurement independence\"\n- The paper covers 3 tasks × 2 models but originally planned more (DocQA abandoned, Gemma-2/Llama not attempted)\n- The paper has 14 references\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the CSS framework? Is it essentially majority voting on binary circuit membership?\n   - Is the \"consensus = intersection\" idea obvious or does the formalization add value?\n   - What is the incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. [2025] who also compared methods?\n   - Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?\n   - Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?\n   - Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**\n   - Are there missing references? Consider: ensemble methods in ML more broadly, voting classifiers, Bayesian model combination, other circuit discovery papers\n   - Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair and accurate?\n   - Should there be discussion of: faithfulness metrics beyond CPR/CMD, other ways to combine circuit outputs (union, weighted), existing diagnostics in mechanistic interpretability?\n   - The paper has only 14 references — is this adequate for the breadth of related work?\n\n3. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**\n   - For each of the 4 claims, assess: Is the evidence sufficient? Is the claim appropriately calibrated?\n   - Is \"diagnostic framework\" framing a strength (honest/modest) or weakness (low ambition)?\n   - Does the paper deliver on its stated contributions?\n\n4. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper well-positioned within the mechanistic interpretability literature?\n   - Does the paper make clear what practitioners should actually do with CSS?\n   - Is the narrative coherent: does the paper tell a clear story from motivation to results?\n   - Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery in practice?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n7. **Score and Confidence**\n   - Overall score (1-6)\n   - Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n   - Brief justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider whether the diagnostic/analytical framing is genuinely useful to the mechanistic interpretability community, even if the method itself is simple.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"efbaa113-dca8-4892-8880-01ac32100012","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:28:25.343Z"}
