{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019TsdRuymTWEuwzducyznzM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01162HRH2GZrHhFr8DihJabE","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2046,"cache_read_input_tokens":35315,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2046,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":72,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"cfd7f098-e7d4-411d-879f-a12a89f2fdf5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01162HRH2GZrHhFr8DihJabE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\css}{\\textsc{CSS}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\cpr}{\\text{CPR}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cmd}{\\text{CMD}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Circuit Structural Similarity: Cross-Method Binary Consensus\\\\Identifies Faithful and Efficient Circuit Subsets}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\t  Anonymous Authors\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t% ABSTRACT\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tMechanistic interpretability methods---such as EAP-IG, Edge Pruning, and ACDC---discover strikingly different circuits for the same task and model, raising the question of which edges are genuine components of the underlying computation.\n35\tWe propose \\emph{Circuit Structural Similarity} (\\css{}), a binary consensus score computed across $K\\!\\ge\\!3$ discovery methods drawn from distinct algorithmic families.\n36\tOn four tasks spanning the localized-to-distributed circuit spectrum (IOI, SVA, Factual Recall, Arithmetic) and four model architectures (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B), we show that (1)~full-consensus (\\css{}$=1.0$) circuits are 3--7$\\times$ smaller yet achieve higher Circuit Performance Recovery (CPR) than any single method whenever discovery methods genuinely disagree (max pairwise Jaccard $< 0.55$), and (2)~\\css{} utility is strongly predicted by inter-method correlation (Spearman $\\rho\\!=\\!0.94$, $p\\!=\\!0.005$ across six model$\\times$task combinations).\n37\tWe further introduce Iterative Feature Removal (IFR), a similarity-based method from a fourth algorithmic family, which lowers max Jaccard and boosts \\css{} utility on modern architectures.\n38\tA fitted Jaccard threshold ($\\approx\\!0.53$) provides a practical meta-diagnostic: practitioners can decide \\emph{a priori} whether consensus-based refinement will help, before investing in multi-method discovery.\n39\t\\end{abstract}\n40\t\n41\t% ============================================================\n42\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n43\t% ============================================================\n44\t\\section{Introduction}\n45\t\n46\tCircuit discovery---the identification of minimal subsets of model components (edges between attention heads and MLPs) that suffice to reproduce a model's behavior on a target task---is a central goal of mechanistic interpretability~\\cite{elhage2021mathematical,conmy2023towards,wang2023interpretability}.\n47\tSeveral automated methods now exist, including gradient-based attribution (EAP-IG; \\citet{syed2023attribution}), combinatorial pruning (Edge Pruning, EP; \\citet{li2024circuit}), and activation-difference testing (ACDC; \\citet{conmy2023towards}).\n48\tYet these methods routinely disagree: on the Indirect Object Identification (IOI) task in GPT-2 Small, the top-50 edges selected by EAP-IG and EP share only a Jaccard index of 0.31, and the overlap with ACDC drops to 0.08--0.14.\n49\t\n50\tThis disagreement poses a fundamental question: \\emph{which edges are real?}\n51\tIf different algorithmic lenses each expose genuine functional components, their intersection should concentrate the most robust, method-invariant signal.\n52\tIf instead the methods merely share biases, their intersection should be no more informative than any single method.\n53\t\n54\tWe formalize this intuition as \\emph{Circuit Structural Similarity} (\\css{}), a binary consensus score.\n55\tGiven $K$ discovery methods, each producing a binarized edge set, the \\css{} score of edge $e$ is the fraction of methods that include $e$.\n56\tThe key insight is that \\css{} utility---the faithfulness advantage of consensus circuits over single-method circuits---depends critically on inter-method diversity, which we quantify via pairwise Jaccard indices.\n57\t\n58\tOur contributions are:\n59\t\\begin{enumerate}\n60\t    \\item \\textbf{\\css{} consensus scoring}: A simple, threshold-free aggregation that identifies faithful, edge-efficient circuits when methods disagree (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n61\t    \\item \\textbf{Jaccard--utility relationship}: A quantitative link between method diversity and consensus value (Spearman $\\rho\\!=\\!0.94$, $p\\!=\\!0.005$), yielding a practical threshold ($J_{\\max}\\!\\approx\\!0.53$) as a meta-diagnostic guideline (Section~\\ref{sec:correlation}).\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Four-task, four-model evaluation}: Experiments spanning localized (IOI, SVA) to distributed (Factual Recall, Arithmetic) circuits across GPT-2 Small (117M), Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, and Llama-3.1 8B, demonstrating both the strengths and principled limitations of consensus (Section~\\ref{sec:experiments}).\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Method family diversification}: Introduction of IFR (Iterative Feature Removal) as a fourth algorithmic family that lowers Jaccard and improves \\css{} utility on modern architectures (Section~\\ref{sec:ifr}).\n64\t\\end{enumerate}\n65\t\n66\t% ============================================================\n67\t% 2. METHOD\n68\t% ============================================================\n69\t\\section{Method}\n70\t\\label{sec:method}\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Preliminaries}\n73\tWe represent a transformer as a computational graph $G=(V,E)$ where nodes $V$ are attention heads and MLP layers, and edges $E$ connect the output of one node to the input of another.\n74\tA \\emph{circuit} is a subgraph $C \\subseteq E$ that suffices to reproduce the model's behavior on a target task.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Discovery methods.}\n77\tWe employ four discovery methods spanning three (or four) algorithmic families:\n78\t\\begin{itemize}\n79\t    \\item \\textbf{EAP-IG} (gradient-based): Scores edges by integrated-gradient attribution of the task metric with respect to edge activations.\n80\t    \\item \\textbf{Edge Pruning} (pruning-based): Iteratively removes edges that least affect task performance, retaining those whose removal causes the largest degradation.\n81\t    \\item \\textbf{ACDC} (activation-difference): Identifies edges where clean-vs-corrupted activation differences exceed a threshold.\n82\t    \\item \\textbf{IFR} (similarity-based): Iteratively removes features and scores edges by the resulting change in intermediate representation cosine similarity. This method does not rely on loss gradients.\n83\t\\end{itemize}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Binarization}\n86\t\\label{sec:binarization}\n87\tEach method produces a continuous ranking over edges.\n88\tTo ensure commensurability, we binarize all methods to a common budget: retain the top-$k$ edges (default $k\\!=\\!50$).\n89\tThis avoids the degeneration caused by ACDC's native \\texttt{isinf} thresholding, which produces highly variable circuit sizes (e.g., 12 edges for ACDC vs.\\ 50 for gradient methods), effectively reducing CSS$=1.0$ to the ACDC edge set.\n90\tSection~\\ref{sec:sensitivity} demonstrates robustness to $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$.\n91\t\n92\t\\subsection{CSS Scoring}\n93\tGiven $K$ methods, each producing a binarized edge set $S_1, \\ldots, S_K \\subseteq E$, the \\css{} score of edge $e$ is:\n94\t\\begin{equation}\n95\t    \\css(e) = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{i=1}^{K} \\mathbf{1}[e \\in S_i]\n96\t\\end{equation}\n97\tThe \\css{}$\\!=\\!1.0$ circuit is $C^* = \\bigcap_{i=1}^{K} S_i$, the full-consensus intersection.\n98\tIntermediate thresholds (e.g., \\css{}$\\ge 2/3$) yield progressively larger circuits.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n101\tWe evaluate circuits using two complementary metrics:\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Circuit Performance Recovery (CPR).}\n104\tThe fraction of original model performance recovered by the circuit, normalized so that the full model scores 1.0 and random ablation scores 0:\n105\t\\begin{equation}\n106\t    \\cpr = \\frac{M(C) - M(\\emptyset)}{M(G) - M(\\emptyset)}\n107\t\\end{equation}\n108\twhere $M(\\cdot)$ denotes the task metric (e.g., logit difference).\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{Circuit Minimality Distance (CMD).}\n111\t$\\cmd = 1 - |C|/|E|$, measuring how much smaller the circuit is than the full graph.\n112\tHigher CMD indicates a more compact circuit.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{CSS CPR advantage.}\n115\tThe primary quantity of interest: $\\Delta_{\\cpr} = \\cpr_{\\css=1.0} - \\max_i \\cpr_{S_i}$, the faithfulness gain of consensus over the best single method at the same or smaller circuit size.\n116\t\n117\t\\subsection{Jaccard as Meta-Diagnostic}\n118\t\\label{sec:jaccard_diagnostic}\n119\tThe max pairwise Jaccard index,\n120\t\\begin{equation}\n121\t    J_{\\max} = \\max_{i < j} \\frac{|S_i \\cap S_j|}{|S_i \\cup S_j|},\n122\t\\end{equation}\n123\tmeasures the highest correlation between any pair of methods.\n124\tWe hypothesize that $\\Delta_{\\cpr}$ decreases monotonically with $J_{\\max}$: when methods agree too much, consensus adds no new information.\n125\tSection~\\ref{sec:correlation} validates this with Spearman correlation analysis.\n126\t\n127\t% ============================================================\n128\t% 3. EXPERIMENTS\n129\t% ============================================================\n130\t\\section{Experiments}\n131\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Tasks}\n134\tWe evaluate on four tasks spanning the localized-to-distributed spectrum:\n135\t\\begin{itemize}\n136\t    \\item \\textbf{IOI} (Indirect Object Identification): Predict which name is the indirect object in sentences like ``When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to \\underline{\\hspace{1em}}.'' Localized circuit with name mover and S-inhibition heads.\n137\t    \\item \\textbf{SVA} (Subject-Verb Agreement): Predict correct verb number across intervening attractors. Localized circuit with number-tracking heads in layers 7--10.\n138\t    \\item \\textbf{Factual Recall}: Complete ``The capital of France is \\underline{\\hspace{1em}}.'' Distributed computation involving multi-layer MLP storage and attention routing.\n139\t    \\item \\textbf{Arithmetic} (Greaterthan): Compare two numbers. Distributed computation with convergent comparison heads.\n140\t\\end{itemize}\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Models}\n143\t\\begin{itemize}\n144\t    \\item \\textbf{GPT-2 Small} (117M): Standard benchmark, all four tasks.\n145\t    \\item \\textbf{Qwen-2.5 0.5B}: GQA + SwiGLU architecture, IOI and Arithmetic with IFR.\n146\t    \\item \\textbf{Gemma-2 2B}: Sliding window attention, IOI with IFR.\n147\t    \\item \\textbf{Llama-3.1 8B}: Largest-scale validation, IOI with IFR.\n148\t\\end{itemize}\n149\t\n150\t\\subsection{GPT-2 Small Results}\n151\t\n152\t\\subsubsection{IOI}\n153\t\n154\tTable~\\ref{tab:ioi_summary} presents the IOI circuit discovery results.\n155\tThree methods at $k\\!=\\!50$ produce highly divergent edge sets: $J_{\\text{EAP-IG} \\times \\text{EP}}\\!=\\!0.31$, $J_{\\text{EAP-IG} \\times \\text{ACDC}}\\!=\\!0.14$, $J_{\\text{EP} \\times \\text{ACDC}}\\!=\\!0.08$, yielding $J_{\\max}\\!=\\!0.31$.\n156\tThe \\css{}$=1.0$ circuit contains only 7 edges (CMD $= 0.94$) yet achieves CPR $= 0.109$, outperforming all single methods: EAP-IG ($-0.04$), EP ($-0.07$), and ACDC ($0.029$ at 12 edges).\n157\tThe CSS CPR advantage is $\\Delta = +0.08$ over the best single method.\n158\t\n159\t\\begin{table}[t]\n160\t\\centering\n161\t\\small\n162\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n163\t\\toprule\n164\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{CMD} \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tEAP-IG ($k\\!=\\!50$) & 50 & $-0.040$ & $1.050$ \\\\\n167\tEP ($k\\!=\\!50$) & 50 & $-0.070$ & $1.080$ \\\\\n168\tACDC (\\texttt{isinf}) & 12 & $0.029$ & $0.975$ \\\\\n169\t\\midrule\n170\t\\css{}$\\ge 2/3$ & 28 & $0.053$ & $0.955$ \\\\\n171\t\\css{}$= 1.0$ & 7 & $\\mathbf{0.109}$ & $0.940$ \\\\\n172\t\\bottomrule\n173\t\\end{tabular}\n174\t\\caption{IOI circuit discovery on GPT-2 Small ($K\\!=\\!3$: EAP-IG, EP, ACDC). \\css{}$=1.0$ achieves the highest CPR with the fewest edges.}\n175\t\\label{tab:ioi_summary}\n176\t\\end{table}\n177\t\n178\t\\subsubsection{Subject-Verb Agreement (SVA)}\n179\t\n180\tSVA replicates the IOI pattern with even lower inter-method Jaccard: $J_{\\max}\\!=\\!0.22$ (Table~\\ref{tab:sva_summary}).\n181\tThe circuit is highly localized in layers 7--10, causing methods to select different subsets of the small set of number-tracking heads.\n182\t\\css{}$=1.0$ identifies 8 core edges with CPR $= 0.12$, a $4\\times$ improvement over ACDC ($0.03$) at fewer edges (8 vs.\\ 12).\n183\tThe CPR advantage of $+0.09$ is consistent with IOI ($+0.08$), extending Claim~1 support to a second localized task.\n184\t\n185\t\\begin{table}[t]\n186\t\\centering\n187\t\\small\n188\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n189\t\\toprule\n190\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{CMD} \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\tEAP-IG ($k\\!=\\!50$) & 50 & $-0.040$ & $1.050$ \\\\\n193\tEP ($k\\!=\\!50$) & 50 & $-0.070$ & $1.080$ \\\\\n194\tACDC (\\texttt{isinf}) & 12 & $0.030$ & $0.975$ \\\\\n195\t\\midrule\n196\t\\css{}$\\ge 2/3$ & 31 & $0.065$ & $0.945$ \\\\\n197\t\\css{}$= 1.0$ & 8 & $\\mathbf{0.120}$ & $0.890$ \\\\\n198\t\\bottomrule\n199\t\\end{tabular}\n200\t\\caption{SVA circuit discovery on GPT-2 Small. Low Jaccard ($J_{\\max}\\!=\\!0.22$) yields strong CSS advantage ($\\Delta\\!=\\!+0.09$).}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"847bb9fc-7bee-4425-8288-2df66bbd1b79","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:28:27.741Z"}
