{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0147aWcaNi3TA1he7P2CunUQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016wHA9gZVuqQS3UJjXiNY3V","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/median/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2208,"cache_read_input_tokens":64781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2208,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"2019fa39-75cb-4057-b63c-986451d838d4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016wHA9gZVuqQS3UJjXiNY3V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n10\t\n11\t\\newcommand{\\css}{\\textsc{CSS}}\n12\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n13\t\\newcommand{\\xmark}{$\\times$}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree?\\\\Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework\\\\for Mechanistic Interpretability}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\tMechanistic interpretability relies on circuit discovery methods to identify minimal subgraphs that explain model behavior, yet different methods---Edge Attribution Patching (EAP-IG), Edge Pruning (EP), ACDC, and Integrated Feature Replacement (IFR)---routinely identify divergent circuits for the same task.\n25\tWe introduce \\emph{Circuit Structure Consensus} (\\css{}), a framework that scores each edge by the fraction of $K\\!\\geq\\!3$ discovery methods that independently include it.\n26\tFull-consensus edges (\\css{}$=1.0$) form dramatically smaller circuits (3--5$\\times$ fewer edges) with comparable or superior faithfulness to any single method---but only when methods provide genuinely independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard similarity).\n27\tWhen methods are highly correlated, consensus degenerates to the most selective method.\n28\tAcross five model--task configurations (GPT-2 Small and Qwen-2.5 0.5B on IOI, SVA, and Greater-Than), we observe a negative correlation between maximum pairwise Jaccard and \\css{} advantage (Spearman $\\rho=-0.90$, $p=0.037$), identifying an approximate Jaccard threshold (${\\sim}0.45$) below which consensus adds value.\n29\tWe further diagnose a binarization pathology in ACDC's default threshold, where \\css{}$=1.0$ collapses to the ACDC circuit exactly, and show that top-$k$ normalization corrects this degeneration.\n30\tFinally, we demonstrate that \\css{}'s binary consensus is more stable than score-based circuit ensembling (CIRCUS), which produces negative-faithfulness circuits due to threshold sensitivity.\n31\tOur results frame \\css{} as a diagnostic tool: a practitioner-facing framework for assessing when cross-method agreement is meaningful and when it is an artifact of method correlation.\n32\t\\end{abstract}\n33\t\n34\t%======================================================================\n35\t\\section{Introduction}\n36\t\\label{sec:intro}\n37\t\n38\tCircuit discovery---the identification of minimal computational subgraphs that explain specific model behaviors---has become a central methodology in mechanistic interpretability \\cite{elhage2021mathematical,conmy2023towards,wang2023interpretability}.\n39\tMultiple algorithmic approaches have been proposed, including gradient-based attribution (EAP-IG; \\cite{syed2023attribution}), activation patching (Edge Pruning; \\cite{bhaskar2024finding}), iterative search (ACDC; \\cite{conmy2023towards}), and representation similarity (IFR; \\cite{hanna2024have}).\n40\tA growing body of work has documented that these methods frequently identify substantially different circuits for the same task \\cite{mondorf2025from}, raising a fundamental question: which edges are ``real''?\n41\t\n42\tOne natural response is to seek consensus across methods: edges identified by multiple independent approaches are more likely to reflect genuine computational structure than artifacts of any single method's inductive bias.\n43\tHowever, this intuition carries a crucial prerequisite---methods must provide \\emph{independent} evidence.\n44\tWhen discovery methods share algorithmic assumptions (e.g., gradient-based sensitivity), their agreement may reflect shared bias rather than ground truth.\n45\t\n46\tWe formalize this intuition as \\emph{Circuit Structure Consensus} (\\css{}), a simple framework that scores each edge by the fraction of $K$ discovery methods that include it in their top-$k$ circuits.\n47\t\\css{} serves two roles:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep]\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit extraction}: full-consensus edges (\\css{}$=1.0$) form compact, faithful circuits when methods genuinely disagree;\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Diagnostic tool}: the \\css{} distribution across an edge population reveals whether methods are providing independent or redundant evidence.\n51\t\\end{enumerate}\n52\t\n53\tOur experimental investigation across three tasks (Indirect Object Identification, Subject-Verb Agreement, Greater-Than Arithmetic) on two model architectures (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-2.5 0.5B) yields the following findings:\n54\t\n55\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep]\n56\t    \\item \\css{}$=1.0$ circuits achieve comparable faithfulness to individual methods using 3--5$\\times$ fewer edges, but this advantage holds only when maximum pairwise Jaccard is below ${\\sim}0.45$ (\\S\\ref{sec:faithfulness}).\n57\t    \\item An observed negative correlation between method agreement (Jaccard) and \\css{} advantage provides a practical diagnostic for when consensus is valuable (Spearman $\\rho = -0.90$, though $n\\!=\\!5$ limits statistical power; \\S\\ref{sec:jaccard}).\n58\t    \\item ACDC's default binarization threshold (\\texttt{isinf}) causes a degeneration where \\css{}$=1.0$ collapses to the ACDC circuit; top-$k$ normalization corrects this (\\S\\ref{sec:binarization}).\n59\t    \\item Binary consensus (\\css{}) is strictly more stable than score-based circuit ensembling (CIRCUS), which produces negative-faithfulness circuits due to score calibration failure (\\S\\ref{sec:circus}).\n60\t\\end{enumerate}\n61\t\n62\tWe frame \\css{} as an analytical and diagnostic framework rather than claiming universal superiority.\n63\tThe primary contribution is methodological: providing practitioners with tools to assess \\emph{when} cross-method agreement is meaningful, diagnose binarization artifacts, and understand task-dependent circuit structure through the lens of method consensus.\n64\t\n65\t%======================================================================\n66\t\\section{Related Work}\n67\t\\label{sec:related}\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Circuit discovery.}\n70\tMultiple paradigms exist for extracting circuits from transformer models.\n71\t\\emph{Activation patching} methods \\cite{conmy2023towards,bhaskar2024finding} measure the causal effect of replacing individual edge activations; \\emph{gradient-based attribution} methods \\cite{syed2023attribution,marks2024sparse} score edges by integrated gradient norms; and \\emph{representation similarity} methods \\cite{hanna2024have} identify edges whose removal maximally changes internal representations.\n72\tEach carries distinct inductive biases: gradient methods favor smooth importance landscapes, patching methods depend on the choice of counterfactual distribution, and ACDC's iterative search is sensitive to its binarization threshold.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Cross-method comparison.}\n75\tMondorf et al.\\ \\cite{mondorf2025from} systematically compared circuit discovery methods, finding low agreement and proposing score averaging within the gradient family.\n76\tOur work differs in two ways: we use binary consensus rather than score averaging (avoiding cross-method score calibration), and we include methods from different algorithmic families.\n77\tPIE \\cite{huo2024pie} proposes iterative pruning ensembles but operates within a single paradigm.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Circuit ensembling.}\n80\tCIRCUS \\cite{parekh2026circus} uses threshold diversity \\emph{within} a single method to create ensemble circuits.\n81\tWe show that this within-method approach fails where cross-method consensus succeeds (\\S\\ref{sec:circus}), because threshold variation does not provide genuinely independent evidence.\n82\t\n83\t%======================================================================\n84\t\\section{Circuit Structure Consensus}\n85\t\\label{sec:method}\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Setup and Notation}\n88\t\n89\tConsider a transformer model $\\mathcal{M}$ with computational graph $G = (V, E)$, where nodes $V$ are attention heads and MLP layers, and edges $E$ represent connections between them.\n90\tA \\emph{circuit discovery method} $m$ produces a binary circuit $C_m \\subseteq E$ for a given task.\n91\tWe assume access to $K \\geq 3$ methods $\\{m_1, \\ldots, m_K\\}$ from different algorithmic families.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{CSS Definition}\n94\t\n95\tFor each edge $e \\in E$, the \\emph{consensus score} is:\n96\t\\begin{equation}\n97\t    \\css(e) = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{i=1}^{K} \\mathbf{1}[e \\in C_{m_i}]\n98\t\\end{equation}\n99\twhere $\\mathbf{1}[\\cdot]$ is the indicator function.\n100\tThe consensus score takes values in $\\{0, 1/K, 2/K, \\ldots, 1\\}$."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"65c6868a-bbcc-4625-a34e-7ef6808b04f6","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:28:52.637Z"}
