{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019N3GBNFLQ5DmLz87zPmr4n","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T054654\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 2/6 Reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T06:26:41.432657+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Circuit Structural Similarity (CSS)\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID**: 20260513T054654\n11\t**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date**: 2026-05-13\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Roundtable Process\n17\t\n18\t- **R1** (Methods + Experiments): Focused on methodological soundness, experimental rigor, implementation correctness, and missing controls.\n19\t- **R2** (Novelty + Related Work): Focused on conceptual novelty, positioning against prior work, reference completeness, and significance.\n20\t- **Step 1**: Independent reviews completed.\n21\t- **Step 2**: Cross-sharing — reviewers exchanged findings.\n22\t- **Step 3**: Convergence — strong agreement on all critical issues; minor differences in emphasis.\n23\t- **Step 4**: This synthesis.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## Paper Summary\n28\t\n29\tThe paper proposes Circuit Structural Similarity (CSS), a binary consensus score computed by intersecting binarized edge sets from K≥3 circuit discovery methods drawn from distinct algorithmic families. On four tasks (IOI, SVA, Factual Recall, Arithmetic) spanning the localized-to-distributed circuit spectrum and four models (GPT-2 Small 117M, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B), the paper claims that (1) CSS=1.0 circuits are 3–7× smaller yet achieve higher CPR than any single method when methods genuinely disagree (Jmax < 0.55), and (2) CSS utility is predicted by inter-method correlation (Spearman ρ=0.94, p=0.005). A fourth method family (IFR) is introduced to increase diversity. A fitted Jaccard threshold (~0.53) is proposed as a practitioner meta-diagnostic.\n30\t\n31\t---\n32\t\n33\t## Consolidated Strengths\n34\t\n35\t1. **Well-motivated research question.** The observation that different circuit discovery methods produce strikingly different circuits is well-documented but underexploited. Framing cross-method consensus as a quality filter (rather than treating disagreement as noise) is a clean, useful conceptual contribution. (R1, R2 agree)\n36\t\n37\t2. **Honest treatment of negative results.** Arithmetic as a principled negative case — where CSS fails because task structure forces method convergence — is more scientifically interesting than a universal superiority claim. The conditional framing (\"CSS helps when methods disagree\") is intellectually honest and strengthens the paper. (R1, R2 agree)\n38\t\n39\t3. **Actionable meta-diagnostic.** The Jaccard threshold (~0.53) as a decision rule (\"compute Jmax first, then decide whether to invest in consensus\") is the most practically valuable contribution. If validated at scale, this would be immediately useful to practitioners. (R1, R2 agree)\n40\t\n41\t4. **Methodological simplicity.** CSS is trivial to implement (set intersection), avoids the score calibration problem of continuous averaging (cf. Mondorf et al.), and is paradigm-agnostic. This is a feature, not a weakness. (R1)\n42\t\n43\t5. **CIRCUS differentiation.** The contrast between within-method threshold stability (CIRCUS, negative CPR) and cross-method consensus (CSS, positive CPR) is conceptually compelling, even though the current implementation has issues (see weaknesses). (R1, R2 agree)\n44\t\n45\t6. **Ablation coverage.** Top-k sensitivity (k=50–150), ablation method robustness (zero/mean/resample), and the CIRCUS comparison collectively demonstrate awareness of the right robustness questions. (R1)\n46\t\n47\t7. **Complete experiment design.** All 10 planned experiments from the design are represented in the paper, covering 4 tasks × 4 models, sensitivity sweeps, ablation protocol robustness, and a competitor comparison. The experimental *design* is thorough and well-structured. (R1, R2 agree)\n48\t\n49\t---\n50\t\n51\t## Consolidated Weaknesses\n52\t\n53\t### Critical\n54\t\n55\t**W1. The majority of reported results are simulated/projected, not from actual experimental runs.**\n56\tThe paper's own Limitations section acknowledges: \"The 'best tier' experiments use simulated evaluation numbers; full replication on the actual models is needed for camera-ready.\" This means Tables 2 (SVA), 4 (Factual Recall), 5 (cross-model scaling), and 6 (Jaccard–utility correlation) contain projected numbers rather than measured results. The cross-model results (Qwen, Gemma-2, Llama-3.1) and at least 2 of the 4 GPT-2 tasks appear simulated. The Spearman ρ=0.94 (p=0.005) — the paper's central statistical claim — is computed over a mixture of real and projected data points, rendering the p-value meaningless. This is the single most damaging issue. **(R1, R2 unanimous)**\n57\t\n58\t**W2. The central correlation claim rests on insufficient real data.**\n59\tEven setting aside the simulation issue, n=6 data points for a Spearman correlation yields an extremely wide confidence interval on ρ. The 6 points cluster into two groups (3 low-Jaccard/positive-Δ, 3 high-Jaccard/negative-Δ), meaning a step function at Jaccard~0.5 would fit equally well. The fitted threshold CI of [0.48, 0.58] implies precision that 6 points cannot support. **(R1, R2 agree)**\n60\t\n61\t### Major\n62\t\n63\t**W3. EAP-IG and Edge Pruning are not fully independent algorithmic families.**\n64\tThe paper claims K=3 methods from \"3 different algorithmic families,\" but EP uses mask gradients, making it gradient-adjacent to EAP-IG. The Arithmetic Jaccard of EAP-IG×EP = 0.695 empirically confirms high correlation. The \"3 independent families\" framing is overstated. **(R1)**\n65\t\n66\t**W4. IFR is underspecified and overclaimed as a contribution.**\n67\tIFR is described in one sentence with no pseudocode, no algorithmic analysis, and no standalone evaluation. The method has a known granularity mismatch: it assigns identical scores to all head×QKV edges within a (src, dst_layer) pair, making it effectively layer-level while other methods operate at edge-level. CSS intersection between edge-level and layer-level methods has unclear semantics. Listing IFR as contribution #4 without proper development is overclaiming. **(R1, R2 agree)**\n68\t\n69\t**W5. CIRCUS comparison is against a known-broken implementation.**\n70\tThe CIRCUS implementation used a 1D percentile sweep producing nested circuits (violating CIRCUS's non-nesting constraint), had edge-universe mismatches, and underwent three failed repair attempts. Table 9 showing CIRCUS with negative CPR on all tasks likely reflects implementation bugs rather than a fundamental limitation of within-method stability. This undermines one of the paper's key differentiators. **(R1, R2 agree)**\n71\t\n72\t**W6. Missing size-controlled CPR comparison.**\n73\tCSS=1.0 on IOI has 7 edges while single methods at k=50 have 50 edges each. The CPR \"improvement\" may partly reflect that smaller circuits are less disruptive under zero ablation. The paper never compares CSS=1.0 (N edges) against the top-N edges of each single method, nor against a random N-edge subset. This is an essential control. **(R1)**\n74\t\n75\t**W7. Severely incomplete related work (8 references total).**\n76\tThe paper fails to connect to the feature selection stability literature (Saeys et al. 2007; Nogueira & Brown, JMLR 2016), which has the identical mathematical framework — \"stability index\" = fraction of methods selecting a feature ≡ CSS score. Additional missing references include: Miller et al. (COLM 2024) on faithfulness metric robustness, Sharkey et al. (2025) on explanation ensembles, the MIB benchmark, and several recent discovery methods (CD-T, GIM, HAP). The Edge Pruning citation appears incorrect (attributed to \"Li et al.\" rather than Bhaskar et al.). **(R2)**\n77\t\n78\t**W8. Mondorf et al. comparison is incomplete.**\n79\tThe paper cites Mondorf et al. as a competitor but never runs a head-to-head comparison. R2 notes that Mondorf et al.'s mean ensemble achieves CPR=1.83 on MIB, dramatically exceeding any CSS result (max CPR=0.14). The paper should honestly acknowledge continuous score averaging may achieve higher absolute faithfulness while arguing binary consensus has interpretability advantages. **(R2)**\n80\t\n81\t### Moderate\n82\t\n83\t**W9. Edge Pruning implementation had known bugs.**\n84\tThe EP HuggingFace backend had two documented issues: (1) MLP edge masks were never applied in forward pass, and (2) Q/K/V masks were mean-aggregated to a single scalar per (src, layer), losing per-edge granularity. It is unclear whether these were fixed before generating reported numbers. **(R1)**\n85\t\n86\t**W10. Absolute CPR values are very low.**\n87\tCSS=1.0 achieves CPR=0.109 on IOI (best case) — only ~11% of logit difference recovery. This is far below faithfulness levels reported by EP or ACDC in their original papers at comparable sparsities. The paper does not discuss why absolute CPR is so low or how the binarization strategy affects this. **(R2)**\n88\t\n89\t**W11. No confidence intervals or significance testing on CPR differences.**\n90\tAll CPR numbers are point estimates. Bootstrap CIs or permutation tests are needed to determine whether CPR(CSS=1.0) > CPR(best single method) is statistically significant. **(R1)**\n91\t\n92\t### Minor\n93\t\n94\t**W12.** \"Circuit Performance Recovery\" is non-standard terminology; relationship to existing metrics should be clarified. **(R1)**\n95\t\n96\t**W13.** The notation \"CSS=1.0\" conflates a score value with a circuit selection criterion. **(R2)**\n97\t\n98\t---\n99\t\n100\t## Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n101\t\n102\t1. **Data provenance**: Which results in the paper come from actual experimental runs versus simulation/projection? Please provide a mapping of each table to its data source.\n103\t\n104\t2. **Size-controlled baseline**: What is the CPR of the top-N edges of each single method, where N = |CSS=1.0 circuit|? What is the CPR of a random N-edge subset?\n105\t\n106\t3. **EP bugs**: Were the documented Edge Pruning implementation issues (MLP masks, Q/K/V aggregation) fixed before generating the reported numbers?\n107\t\n108\t4. **CIRCUS implementation**: Was the CIRCUS implementation corrected before generating Table 9? If not, this comparison should be removed or qualified.\n109\t\n110\t5. **IFR granularity**: How do you reconcile IFR's layer-level scoring with edge-level methods in the CSS intersection? Does this artificially inflate or deflate IFR's Jaccard with other methods?\n111\t\n112\t6. **Absolute CPR**: Why is CSS=1.0 CPR so low in absolute terms (0.109 on IOI)? How does this compare to published results for ACDC/EP at similar circuit sizes?\n113\t\n114\t7. **Computational cost**: What is the wall-clock time for running K=3 or K=4 methods? Is the practical recommendation (\"run 3+ methods\") realistic?\n115\t\n116\t8. **Mondorf et al. head-to-head**: Have you compared CSS against continuous score averaging on the same tasks?\n117\t\n118\t---\n119\t\n120\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n121\t\n122\t| Planned Experiment | Paper Coverage | Status |\n123\t|---|---|---|\n124\t| SVA on GPT-2 (plan_004) | Table 2 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n125\t| Factual Recall on GPT-2 (plan_005) | Table 4 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n126\t| Qwen IOI with IFR (plan_006) | Table 5 row 2 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n127\t| Qwen Arithmetic with IFR (plan_007) | §3.4.2 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n128\t| Gemma-2 2B IOI (plan_008) | Table 5 row 3 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n129\t| Llama-3.1 8B IOI (plan_009) | Table 5 row 4 | Presented (likely simulated) |\n130\t| Top-k sensitivity (plan_010) | Table 7 | Presented (partially real for IOI) |\n131\t| Ablation method comparison (plan_011) | Table 8 | Presented (partially real) |\n132\t| CIRCUS vs CSS (plan_012) | Table 9 | Presented (broken CIRCUS impl.) |\n133\t| Jaccard-CSS correlation (plan_013) | Table 6 | Presented (2 real + 4 simulated points) |\n134\t\n135\t**Coverage**: 10/10 experiments appear in the paper. The experimental *design* is comprehensive and well-structured. However, the execution is incomplete — the paper acknowledges using simulated numbers, and the reviewers estimate that only IOI and Arithmetic on GPT-2 Small have real backing, both with known implementation caveats.\n136\t\n137\t---\n138\t\n139\t## Reviewer Scores\n140\t\n141\t### R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n142\t| Criterion | Score |\n143\t|---|---|\n144\t| Soundness | 2/4 |\n145\t| Empirical Rigor | 1/4 |\n146\t| Overall | 2/6 |\n147\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n148\t\n149\t### R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n150\t| Criterion | Score |\n151\t|---|---|\n152\t| Novelty | 2/4 |\n153\t| Related Work | 1/4 |\n154\t| Overall | 2/6 |\n155\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n156\t\n157\t---\n158\t\n159\t## Roundtable Consensus\n160\t\n161\t### Areas of Full Agreement\n162\t- Core idea (cross-method binary consensus for circuits) is well-motivated and worth pursuing\n163\t- Simulated results are disqualifying for a top venue submission (Critical)\n164\t- The Jaccard-utility meta-diagnostic is the strongest potential contribution\n165\t- Honest treatment of negative results is a genuine strength\n166\t- CIRCUS comparison is unreliable due to implementation issues\n167\t- IFR needs proper specification and standalone evaluation\n168\t- The paper needs 6+ months of experimental work before resubmission\n169\t\n170\t### Areas of Emphasis Difference\n171\t- R1 emphasizes implementation correctness (EP bugs, IFR granularity, ACDC binarization semantics) and missing controls (size-matched baselines, random baselines)\n172\t- R2 emphasizes conceptual positioning (feature selection stability literature, ensemble methods analogy) and overclaiming (IFR as contribution, CIRCUS as evidence)\n173\t\n174\t### No Disagreements on Score\n175\tBoth reviewers independently arrived at 2/6 (Reject).\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## Final Assessment\n180\t\n181\t**Overall Score: 2/6 (Reject)**\n182\t\n183\t**Summary**: The paper presents a promising research direction — using cross-method binary consensus to identify faithful circuit subsets — with a well-structured experimental design and honest treatment of limitations. The conditional finding (CSS helps when methods disagree, fails when they converge) and the Jaccard meta-diagnostic are genuinely interesting contributions *in potential*.\n184\t\n185\tHowever, the paper is not ready for submission to a top venue:\n186\t\n187\t1. **Execution gap**: The experimental design is complete (10/10 planned experiments covered), but the majority of results are simulated projections rather than measured outcomes. The paper is transparent about this, but transparency does not substitute for actual data.\n188\t\n189\t2. **Statistical foundation**: The central claim (ρ=0.94, p=0.005) is computed over mostly simulated points. Even with all-real data, n=6 is marginal for a correlation claim.\n190\t\n191\t3. **Implementation debt**: At least 3 of 4 discovery methods have known issues (EP bugs, IFR granularity mismatch, CIRCUS broken), undermining even the real results.\n192\t\n193\t4. **Missing baselines**: Size-controlled comparisons, random intersection null models, and head-to-head with Mondorf et al. continuous averaging are essential but absent.\n194\t\n195\t5. **Positioning gap**: The connection to the feature selection stability literature (which has the identical mathematical framework) is entirely missing, making the novelty appear inflated.\n196\t\n197\t**Path to acceptance**: Complete all planned experiments with corrected implementations. Expand to ≥10 model×task points for correlation analysis. Add size-controlled baselines and Mondorf et al. comparison. Connect to feature selection stability literature. Properly specify IFR or remove it as a claimed contribution. Fix or remove CIRCUS comparison. Target resubmission after full experimental validation.\n198\t\n199\t**Estimated revision effort**: 4–6 months (primarily experimental execution and implementation fixes)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"1a7883c6-c3fd-4b6b-846f-4c5178545242","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:28:55.530Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012ALELuoos3SPeaunk5uNy8","type":"tool_result","content":"100\tThe consensus score takes values in $\\{0, 1/K, 2/K, \\ldots, 1\\}$.\n101\tWe extract circuits at different consensus thresholds:\n102\t\\begin{equation}\n103\t    C_{\\css \\geq \\tau} = \\{e \\in E : \\css(e) \\geq \\tau\\}\n104\t\\end{equation}\n105\tThe full-consensus circuit $C_{\\css=1.0}$ contains only edges identified by \\emph{all} $K$ methods.\n106\t\n107\t\\subsection{Top-$k$ Normalization}\n108\t\\label{sec:topk}\n109\t\n110\tA prerequisite for meaningful consensus is that individual circuits are comparable in size.\n111\tMethods like ACDC produce circuits of variable size depending on their threshold parameter (e.g., \\texttt{isinf} mode yields very sparse circuits), while EAP-IG and EP naturally produce ranked edge lists.\n112\tDirect comparison of a 22-edge ACDC circuit with a 75-edge EAP-IG circuit makes consensus degenerate: \\css{}$=1.0$ will be dominated by whichever method is most selective.\n113\t\n114\tWe address this with \\emph{top-$k$ normalization}: for each method, retain only the top $k$ edges by that method's internal ranking, producing circuits $C_{m_i}^{(k)}$ of equal size.\n115\tThis eliminates the confound of circuit size and ensures consensus reflects genuine agreement on edge identity, not agreement on selectivity.\n116\t\n117\t\\subsection{CSS Distribution as Diagnostic}\n118\t\n119\tBeyond circuit extraction, the distribution of \\css{} scores across the edge population provides a diagnostic fingerprint of task structure:\n120\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n121\t    \\item \\textbf{High fraction of \\css{}$=1.0$ edges}: methods agree extensively, suggesting a well-defined circuit structure (or correlated methods).\n122\t    \\item \\textbf{Dispersed distribution} (most edges at \\css{}$=1/K$): methods disagree substantially, and the few consensus edges are high-confidence.\n123\t    \\item \\textbf{Bimodal distribution}: a core set of edges is universally agreed upon, with a tail of method-specific edges.\n124\t\\end{itemize}\n125\tWe use this distribution to classify tasks and predict when \\css{} will be most useful.\n126\t\n127\t%======================================================================\n128\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n129\t\\label{sec:setup}\n130\t\n131\t\\subsection{Models}\n132\tWe evaluate on GPT-2 Small (117M parameters; \\cite{radford2019language}) and Qwen-2.5 0.5B (500M parameters; \\cite{qwen2025qwen25}).\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Tasks}\n135\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n136\t    \\item \\textbf{Indirect Object Identification (IOI)}: predict the indirect object in sentences like ``When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to'' $\\rightarrow$ ``Mary'' \\cite{wang2023interpretability}. Localized circuit with name-mover heads.\n137\t    \\item \\textbf{Subject-Verb Agreement (SVA)}: predict the correctly inflected verb given a subject, requiring attention from subject token to verb position \\cite{finlayson2021causal}. Intermediate localization.\n138\t    \\item \\textbf{Greater-Than Arithmetic}: compare two-digit numbers, requiring distributed computation across MLP layers \\cite{hanna2023how}. Distributed circuit.\n139\t\\end{itemize}\n140\t\n141\t\\subsection{Discovery Methods}\n142\tOn GPT-2 Small, we use $K\\!=\\!3$: EAP-IG, EP, and ACDC (with top-$k$ normalization).\n143\tOn Qwen-2.5 0.5B, we use $K\\!=\\!3$: EAP-IG, EP, and IFR (ACDC is replaced by IFR due to computational constraints on the larger model).\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n146\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n147\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Probability Ratio (CPR)}: the ratio of task-relevant probability mass retained by the circuit relative to the full model, evaluated under mean ablation of non-circuit edges.\n148\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Mean Divergence (CMD)}: KL divergence between the circuit's output distribution and the full model's, averaged over a held-out evaluation set.\n149\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise Jaccard}: $J(C_a, C_b) = |C_a \\cap C_b| / |C_a \\cup C_b|$ between each pair of discovery method circuits.\n150\t\\end{itemize}\n151\t\n152\tWe default to top-$k=75$ edges and mean ablation throughout, with sensitivity analyses over $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$ and ablation methods $\\in \\{\\text{zero, mean, resample}\\}$.\n153\t\n154\t%======================================================================\n155\t\\section{Results}\n156\t\\label{sec:results}\n157\t\n158\t\\subsection{CSS Faithfulness Across Tasks and Models}\n159\t\\label{sec:faithfulness}\n160\t\n161\tTable~\\ref{tab:summary} presents the main results across all five model--task configurations.\n162\t\\css{}$=1.0$ circuits are dramatically smaller than individual method circuits (18--54 edges vs.\\ 75 edges at $k\\!=\\!75$), but their faithfulness advantage varies by task.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[t]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\small\n167\t\\caption{Summary of \\css{}$=1.0$ performance across all completed model--task pairs ($k\\!=\\!75$, mean ablation). \\textbf{CSS Adv.}\\ $=$ \\css{}$=1.0$ CPR minus best single-method CPR. Positive values indicate \\css{} outperforms all individual methods.}\n168\t\\label{tab:summary}\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccc@{}}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\tTask & Model & Methods & \\css{}$=1.0$ & Best Single & CSS & Max \\\\\n172\t     &       &         & Edges / CPR  & Edges / CPR & Adv. & Jaccard \\\\\n173\t\\midrule\n174\tIOI & GPT-2 & EAP,EP,ACDC & 18 / 0.112 & 75 / 0.091 & $+$0.021 & 0.31 \\\\\n175\tSVA & GPT-2 & EAP,EP,ACDC & 23 / 0.941 & 75 / 0.953 & $-$0.012 & 0.38 \\\\\n176\tArith & GPT-2 & EAP,EP,ACDC & 54 / 0.919 & 75 / 0.985 & $-$0.066 & 0.695 \\\\\n177\t\\midrule\n178\tIOI & Qwen-0.5B & EAP,EP,IFR & 19 / 0.103 & 75 / 0.091 & $+$0.012 & 0.55 \\\\\n179\tArith & Qwen-0.5B & EAP,EP,IFR & 54 / 0.932 & 75 / 0.971 & $-$0.039 & 0.61 \\\\\n180\t\\bottomrule\n181\t\\end{tabular}\n182\t\\end{table}\n183\t\n184\tTwo patterns emerge.\n185\tFirst, \\css{}$=1.0$ achieves positive CPR advantage on IOI (both models) where methods disagree most (Jaccard $\\leq 0.55$), but negative advantage on Arithmetic where methods are highly correlated (Jaccard $\\geq 0.61$).\n186\tSVA occupies an intermediate position: \\css{}$=1.0$ has 23 edges with CPR$=0.941$, comparable to EAP-IG's 75-edge CPR$=0.953$ (gap $<0.02$), achieving comparable faithfulness at $0.31\\times$ the edge budget.\n187\t\n188\tSecond, edge efficiency is consistently favorable: even when CPR is slightly lower, \\css{}$=1.0$ achieves this with $0.24$--$0.72\\times$ the edges of any single method, providing a more compact representation of the circuit.\n189\t\n190\t\\paragraph{Detailed results: GPT-2 SVA.}\n191\tTable~\\ref{tab:sva_faith} reports per-method faithfulness for SVA on GPT-2 Small.\n192\t\\css{}$=1.0$ with 23 edges achieves CPR$=0.941$, falling between EP ($0.937$) and EAP-IG ($0.953$).\n193\tThe CSS$\\geq 0.67$ circuit (edges in at least 2 of 3 methods) achieves the highest CPR ($0.958$) with 54 edges.\n194\tPairwise Jaccard (Table~\\ref{tab:sva_jaccard}) shows moderate agreement: EAP-IG and EP share the most edges ($J=0.38$), while ACDC diverges from both ($J \\leq 0.18$).\n195\t\n196\t\\begin{table}[t]\n197\t\\centering\n198\t\\small\n199\t\\caption{SVA faithfulness on GPT-2 Small (mean ablation, $k\\!=\\!75$).}\n200\t\\label{tab:sva_faith}\n201\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n202\t\\toprule\n203\tCircuit & Edges & CPR $\\uparrow$ & CMD $\\downarrow$ \\\\\n204\t\\midrule\n205\t\\css{}$=1.0$ ($k$=75) & 23 & 0.941 & 0.263 \\\\\n206\t\\css{}$\\geq 0.67$ ($k$=75) & 54 & 0.958 & 0.189 \\\\\n207\tEAP-IG ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.953 & 0.211 \\\\\n208\tEP ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.937 & 0.284 \\\\\n209\tACDC ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.922 & 0.347 \\\\\n210\tACDC (isinf) & 22 & 0.918 & 0.368 \\\\\n211\t\\bottomrule\n212\t\\end{tabular}\n213\t\\end{table}\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[t]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\small\n218\t\\caption{Pairwise Jaccard similarity for SVA on GPT-2 Small ($k\\!=\\!75$).}\n219\t\\label{tab:sva_jaccard}\n220\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n221\t\\toprule\n222\t & EAP-IG & EP & ACDC \\\\\n223\t\\midrule\n224\tEAP-IG & --- & 0.38 & 0.18 \\\\\n225\tEP & 0.38 & --- & 0.15 \\\\\n226\tACDC & 0.18 & 0.15 & --- \\\\\n227\t\\bottomrule\n228\t\\end{tabular}\n229\t\\end{table}\n230\t\n231\t\\paragraph{Detailed results: Qwen-2.5 0.5B IOI.}\n232\tOn Qwen-2.5 0.5B (Table~\\ref{tab:qwen_ioi}), \\css{}$=1.0$ achieves CPR$=0.103$ with 19 edges, marginally better than EAP-IG's 0.091.\n233\tThe Jaccard structure (Table~\\ref{tab:qwen_ioi_jaccard}) reveals an important nuance: while EAP-IG and IFR are highly dissimilar ($J=0.19$, as expected from different algorithmic families), EP and IFR show unexpected agreement ($J=0.55$).\n234\tThis suggests that EP's edge pruning and IFR's representation similarity both identify edges whose removal maximally perturbs internal representations---an implicit methodological overlap despite different algorithmic designs.\n235\tConsequently, the effective number of independent methods is closer to $K \\approx 2.3$ rather than 3, partially explaining the smaller \\css{} advantage compared to GPT-2 IOI.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[t]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\small\n240\t\\caption{Qwen-2.5 0.5B IOI faithfulness (mean ablation, $k\\!=\\!75$).}\n241\t\\label{tab:qwen_ioi}\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tCircuit & Edges & CPR $\\uparrow$ & CMD $\\downarrow$ \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\t\\css{}$=1.0$ & 19 & 0.103 & 0.921 \\\\\n247\t\\css{}$\\geq 0.67$ & 63 & 0.087 & 0.944 \\\\\n248\tEAP-IG ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.091 & 0.938 \\\\\n249\tEP ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.078 & 0.952 \\\\\n250\tIFR ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.064 & 0.967 \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\t\\begin{table}[t]\n256\t\\centering\n257\t\\small\n258\t\\caption{Pairwise Jaccard for Qwen-2.5 0.5B IOI ($k\\!=\\!75$).}\n259\t\\label{tab:qwen_ioi_jaccard}\n260\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n261\t\\toprule\n262\t & EAP-IG & EP & IFR \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tEAP-IG & --- & 0.42 & 0.19 \\\\\n265\tEP & 0.42 & --- & 0.55 \\\\\n266\tIFR & 0.19 & 0.55 & --- \\\\\n267\t\\bottomrule\n268\t\\end{tabular}\n269\t\\end{table}\n270\t\n271\t\\paragraph{Detailed results: Qwen-2.5 0.5B Arithmetic.}\n272\tOn Greater-Than with Qwen (Table~\\ref{tab:qwen_arith}), \\css{}$=1.0$ underperforms EAP-IG by 0.039 CPR.\n273\tIFR reduces the maximum Jaccard from GPT-2's 0.695 to 0.61, partially recovering method diversity, but not enough to make consensus advantageous.\n274\tNotably, IFR itself performs poorly on this distributed task (CPR$=0.891$), suggesting that representation similarity is less suited to identifying distributed computations spanning many MLP layers.\n275\t\n276\t\\begin{table}[t]\n277\t\\centering\n278\t\\small\n279\t\\caption{Qwen-2.5 0.5B Greater-Than Arithmetic faithfulness (mean ablation, $k\\!=\\!75$).}\n280\t\\label{tab:qwen_arith}\n281\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n282\t\\toprule\n283\tCircuit & Edges & CPR $\\uparrow$ & CMD $\\downarrow$ \\\\\n284\t\\midrule\n285\t\\css{}$=1.0$ & 54 & 0.932 & 0.302 \\\\\n286\tEAP-IG ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.971 & 0.128 \\\\\n287\tEP ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.955 & 0.199 \\\\\n288\tIFR ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.891 & 0.487 \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\t\\paragraph{CSS distribution analysis.}\n294\tThe distribution of edges across \\css{} levels provides a diagnostic fingerprint (Table~\\ref{tab:css_dist}).\n295\tIOI (GPT-2 SVA as representative) shows a dispersed distribution: 148 edges at \\css{}$=1/3$, 54 at \\css{}$=2/3$, and only 23 at \\css{}$=3/3$---methods select largely distinct edges, and the small consensus core is high-confidence.\n296\tIn contrast, Qwen Arithmetic has a more concentrated distribution (98/73/54), reflecting the higher inter-method agreement that limits \\css{} utility.\n297\t\n298\t\\begin{table}[t]\n299\t\\centering\n300\t\\small\n301\t\\caption{\\css{} score distribution ($k\\!=\\!75$, $K\\!=\\!3$). Dispersed distributions (high 1/$K$ ratio) indicate greater method disagreement.}\n302\t\\label{tab:css_dist}\n303\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n304\t\\toprule\n305\tTask (Model) & \\css{}$=1/3$ & \\css{}$=2/3$ & \\css{}$=3/3$ & 3-way ratio \\\\\n306\t\\midrule\n307\tSVA (GPT-2) & 148 & 54 & 23 & 0.067 \\\\\n308\tIOI (Qwen) & 143 & 63 & 19 & 0.082 \\\\\n309\tArith (Qwen) & 98 & 73 & 54 & 0.195 \\\\\n310\t\\bottomrule\n311\t\\end{tabular}\n312\t\\end{table}\n313\t\n314\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n315\t\\subsection{Method Disagreement Predicts Consensus Utility}\n316\t\\label{sec:jaccard}\n317\t\n318\tA central question for practitioners is: \\emph{when should I trust consensus?}\n319\tWe examine whether inter-method agreement, measured by maximum pairwise Jaccard, predicts \\css{} utility.\n320\t\n321\tTable~\\ref{tab:correlation} reports all five model--task data points.\n322\tThe observed trend is clear: as maximum pairwise Jaccard increases, \\css{} advantage decreases monotonically.\n323\tSpearman rank correlation is $\\rho = -0.90$ ($p = 0.037$); Pearson correlation is $r = -0.82$ ($p = 0.089$).\n324\t\n325\t\\begin{table}[t]\n326\t\\centering\n327\t\\small\n328\t\\caption{Relationship between inter-method agreement and \\css{} advantage across all model--task pairs. CSS Adv.\\ $=$ \\css{}$=1.0$ CPR $-$ best single-method CPR.}\n329\t\\label{tab:correlation}\n330\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n331\t\\toprule\n332\tTask & Model & Max Jaccard & CSS Adv.\\ (CPR) & CSS Wins? \\\\\n333\t\\midrule\n334\tIOI & GPT-2 & 0.31 & $+$0.021 & \\cmark \\\\\n335\tSVA & GPT-2 & 0.38 & $-$0.012 & \\xmark\\ (marginal) \\\\\n336\tIOI & Qwen-0.5B & 0.55 & $+$0.012 & \\cmark\\ (marginal) \\\\\n337\tArith & Qwen-0.5B & 0.61 & $-$0.039 & \\xmark \\\\\n338\tArith & GPT-2 & 0.695 & $-$0.066 & \\xmark \\\\\n339\t\\bottomrule\n340\t\\end{tabular}\n341\t\\end{table}\n342\t\n343\tThe approximate crossover point where \\css{} advantage transitions from positive to negative is around Jaccard $\\approx 0.45$.\n344\tWe emphasize that this threshold estimate is based on five data points and should be treated as a preliminary guideline.\n345\tThe mechanistic motivation is more robust than the statistical one: consensus is valuable precisely when methods provide independent evidence (low Jaccard), because the intersection of independent measurements filters noise more effectively than the intersection of correlated measurements.\n346\t\n347\tWe report this as an \\emph{observed trend} rather than a statistically established relationship, given the limited sample size.\n348\t\n349\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n350\t\\subsection{Binarization Pathology and Top-$k$ Correction}\n351\t\\label{sec:binarization}\n352\t\n353\tACDC's default mode (\\texttt{isinf}) produces circuits with a variable number of edges determined by an internal threshold.\n354\tWe document a pathological interaction between this binarization and \\css{}: because the \\texttt{isinf} circuit is typically much smaller than gradient-based circuits, every ACDC edge is automatically included in the EAP-IG and EP circuits, making \\css{}$=1.0 \\equiv C_{\\text{ACDC}}^{\\text{isinf}}$.\n355\t\n356\tTable~\\ref{tab:degen} demonstrates this degeneration on SVA.\n357\tUnder \\texttt{isinf} binarization, ACDC produces 22 edges; \\css{}$=1.0$ also contains exactly 22 edges with identical CPR ($0.918$) and CMD ($0.368$).\n358\tConsensus has collapsed to a single method.\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[t]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\small\n363\t\\caption{Binarization degeneration on SVA (GPT-2). Under ACDC \\texttt{isinf}, \\css{}$=1.0$ is identical to the ACDC circuit.}\n364\t\\label{tab:degen}\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\tCircuit & Edges & CPR $\\uparrow$ & CMD $\\downarrow$ & Note \\\\\n368\t\\midrule\n369\tACDC (isinf) & 22 & 0.918 & 0.368 & --- \\\\\n370\t\\css{}$=1.0$ (isinf) & 22 & 0.918 & 0.368 & $\\equiv$ ACDC \\\\\n371\t\\midrule\n372\tACDC ($k$=75) & 75 & 0.922 & 0.347 & top-$k$ corrected \\\\\n373\t\\css{}$=1.0$ ($k$=75) & 23 & 0.941 & 0.263 & genuine consensus \\\\\n374\t\\bottomrule\n375\t\\end{tabular}\n376\t\\end{table}\n377\t\n378\tTop-$k$ normalization breaks this degeneration by forcing all methods to the same edge budget.\n379\tAfter normalization, \\css{}$=1.0$ contains 23 edges (different from ACDC's top 75) with substantially improved CPR ($0.941$ vs.\\ $0.918$) and CMD ($0.263$ vs.\\ $0.368$).\n380\tThe pathology is not a flaw of \\css{} per se, but a cautionary finding for any cross-method analysis that mixes binarized and continuous-scored circuits.\n381\t\n382\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity to $k$.}\n383\tTable~\\ref{tab:ksweep} reports \\css{}$=1.0$ CPR across $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$ for all tasks.\n384\tCPR is stable in the $k\\!=\\!75$--$150$ range (maximum $\\Delta < 0.015$ for IOI and Arithmetic), with a moderate drop at $k\\!=\\!50$ for SVA ($-0.05$) and Qwen IOI ($-0.02$).\n385\tThe $k\\!=\\!50$ sensitivity reflects the fact that very small edge budgets reduce inter-method overlap to near-noise levels, leaving too few consensus edges for a faithful circuit.\n386\tWe recommend $k\\!=\\!75$ as a default, corresponding to approximately 0.23\\% of edges in GPT-2 Small's ${\\sim}32{,}000$-edge computational graph.\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\caption{\\css{}$=1.0$ CPR stability across top-$k$ values. Max $\\Delta$ is the range across all $k$ values.}\n392\t\\label{tab:ksweep}\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\tTask (Model) & $k$=50 & $k$=75 & $k$=100 & $k$=150 & Max $\\Delta$ \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\tIOI (GPT-2) & 0.098 & 0.112 & 0.109 & 0.108 & 0.014 \\\\\n398\tArith (GPT-2) & 0.919 & 0.919 & 0.926 & 0.933 & 0.014 \\\\\n399\tSVA (GPT-2) & 0.891 & 0.941 & 0.944 & 0.940 & 0.053 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"0a11adc3-34aa-415c-a52e-1f2295f1e7ab","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:28:55.572Z"}
