{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FPfg7nGopqe9qbVEHifmxE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R5n4DDNZgGitjFBRgQbxJt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/median/paper.tex","offset":400,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6834,"cache_read_input_tokens":69851,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6834,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"c868416e-307f-44a2-9dcd-28f71855dfd8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R5n4DDNZgGitjFBRgQbxJt","type":"tool_result","content":"400\tIOI (Qwen) & 0.082 & 0.103 & 0.099 & 0.097 & 0.021 \\\\\n401\t\\bottomrule\n402\t\\end{tabular}\n403\t\\end{table}\n404\t\n405\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n406\t\\subsection{CSS vs.\\ Score-Based Ensembling}\n407\t\\label{sec:circus}\n408\t\n409\tCIRCUS \\cite{parekh2026circus} proposes circuit ensembling through threshold diversity within a single method.\n410\tTable~\\ref{tab:circus} compares \\css{} and CIRCUS on IOI (GPT-2, zero ablation).\n411\tCIRCUS's ``core'' circuit---edges consistently selected across thresholds---produces \\emph{negative} CPR ($-0.108$), indicating that the ensemble core actively degrades model behavior.\n412\tBy contrast, \\css{}$=1.0$ achieves positive CPR ($0.109$) with fewer edges (7 vs.\\ 15).\n413\t\n414\t\\begin{table}[t]\n415\t\\centering\n416\t\\small\n417\t\\caption{\\css{} vs.\\ CIRCUS on IOI (GPT-2, zero ablation).}\n418\t\\label{tab:circus}\n419\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n420\t\\toprule\n421\tMethod & Edges & CPR $\\uparrow$ & CMD $\\downarrow$ & Stable across runs? \\\\\n422\t\\midrule\n423\t\\css{}$=1.0$ & 7 & 0.109 & 0.911 & Yes (3/3 identical) \\\\\n424\tCIRCUS core & 15 & $-$0.108 & 1.134 & No (12--18 edges) \\\\\n425\t\\bottomrule\n426\t\\end{tabular}\n427\t\\end{table}\n428\t\n429\tThe failure mode is instructive: CIRCUS relies on faithfulness scoring within a single method to identify core edges, but this scoring is itself sensitive to the ablation method and threshold.\n430\tSmall changes in threshold produce different core sets (12--18 edges across three runs), indicating high instability.\n431\t\\css{}'s binary consensus avoids score calibration entirely: an edge is either in a method's top-$k$ or not, with no need to compare scores across methods or thresholds.\n432\t\n433\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n434\t\\subsection{Ablation Method Robustness}\n435\t\\label{sec:ablation_robust}\n436\t\n437\tWe verify that \\css{} advantage is not an artifact of the evaluation ablation method.\n438\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_method} reports \\css{}$=1.0$ performance on IOI (GPT-2) under three ablation paradigms.\n439\tThe \\css{} advantage is positive under all three, though its magnitude varies: largest under zero ablation ($+0.080$) and smallest under resample ablation ($+0.013$).\n440\tThis is expected: zero ablation maximally damages non-circuit edges, disproportionately benefiting smaller circuits; resample ablation is the most conservative.\n441\t\n442\t\\begin{table}[t]\n443\t\\centering\n444\t\\small\n445\t\\caption{IOI (GPT-2) \\css{} advantage under different evaluation ablation methods.}\n446\t\\label{tab:ablation_method}\n447\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n448\t\\toprule\n449\tAblation Method & \\css{}$=1.0$ CPR & Best Single CPR & Advantage \\\\\n450\t\\midrule\n451\tZero & 0.109 & 0.029 & $+$0.080 \\\\\n452\tMean & 0.112 & 0.091 & $+$0.021 \\\\\n453\tResample & 0.095 & 0.082 & $+$0.013 \\\\\n454\t\\bottomrule\n455\t\\end{tabular}\n456\t\\end{table}\n457\t\n458\tThis analysis is limited to IOI (the only task where the full ablation comparison was completed).\n459\tExtending to all tasks is listed as future work.\n460\t\n461\t%======================================================================\n462\t\\section{Discussion}\n463\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n464\t\n465\t\\paragraph{CSS as diagnostic framework.}\n466\tOur results suggest that \\css{} is most valuable not as a universal circuit extraction method, but as a diagnostic tool for understanding the landscape of circuit discovery.\n467\tThe \\css{} distribution reveals task structure (dispersed for localized tasks like IOI, concentrated for distributed tasks like Arithmetic), and the Jaccard--utility relationship provides practitioners with a simple check: if methods already agree substantially (Jaccard $> 0.45$), consensus adds little beyond the best individual method.\n468\t\n469\t\\paragraph{Method independence is not guaranteed by algorithmic diversity.}\n470\tThe EP--IFR Jaccard of 0.55 on Qwen IOI demonstrates that ``different algorithmic family'' does not guarantee measurement independence.\n471\tBoth EP (activation patching) and IFR (representation similarity) identify edges whose removal maximally changes internal state, creating implicit overlap in what they measure.\n472\tFuture work should develop principled measures of method independence beyond pairwise Jaccard.\n473\t\n474\t\\paragraph{Binarization as a community-wide concern.}\n475\tThe ACDC \\texttt{isinf} degeneration we document is not specific to \\css{}: any analysis comparing ACDC circuits with gradient-based circuits should account for the size mismatch.\n476\tWe recommend that circuit discovery papers report results under standardized edge budgets (e.g., top-$k$) in addition to method-native thresholds.\n477\t\n478\t%======================================================================\n479\t\\section{Limitations}\n480\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n481\t\n482\t\\paragraph{Limited task and model coverage.}\n483\tOur analysis covers three tasks on two small models (117M and 500M parameters).\n484\tA planned fourth task (factual recall/DocQA) was abandoned because GPT-2 Small's performance on factual recall is too weak for ACDC to converge to a meaningful circuit ($<5$ edges).\n485\tScaling to larger models (Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B) was not completed due to computational constraints.\n486\tWhile our three tasks span a range of circuit structures (localized, intermediate, distributed), broader task coverage is needed to establish the generality of our findings.\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{Statistical power.}\n489\tThe Jaccard--utility correlation (Spearman $\\rho = -0.90$, $p = 0.037$) is based on $n = 5$ model--task data points.\n490\tWhile the trend direction is consistent and mechanistically motivated, the statistical test has limited power, and the threshold estimate of Jaccard $\\approx 0.45$ should not be taken as a precise boundary.\n491\t\n492\t\\paragraph{Degree of freedom in top-$k$.}\n493\tThe choice of $k = 75$ is itself a degree of freedom.\n494\tWhile our $k$-sweep shows stability for $k \\in [75, 150]$, a reviewer may reasonably ask whether $k = 75$ was selected post hoc.\n495\tWe chose $k = 75$ based on prior work conventions for GPT-2 Small circuit analysis and verified stability empirically.\n496\t\n497\t\\paragraph{Incomplete ablation comparison.}\n498\tThe ablation method robustness check (zero vs.\\ mean vs.\\ resample) was only completed for IOI on GPT-2.\n499\tWe cannot rule out that the ablation method choice interacts differently with \\css{} on other tasks.\n500\t\n501\t\\paragraph{IFR--EP correlation.}\n502\tOn Qwen IOI, the unexpectedly high IFR--EP Jaccard ($0.55$) reduces the effective number of independent methods.\n503\tThis complicates the interpretation of $K = 3$ consensus on Qwen and suggests that method independence should be verified empirically before applying \\css{}.\n504\t\n505\t%======================================================================\n506\t\\section{Conclusion}\n507\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n508\t\n509\tWe introduced Circuit Structure Consensus (\\css{}), a framework for combining binary circuit discovery outputs across $K \\geq 3$ methods.\n510\tRather than claiming that consensus universally improves circuit quality, we characterize \\emph{when} it helps: when methods provide independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard), \\css{}$=1.0$ circuits are dramatically more edge-efficient and comparably or more faithful.\n511\tWhen methods agree extensively, consensus degenerates to the most selective method.\n512\tWe documented a binarization pathology in ACDC that causes spurious degeneration, proposed top-$k$ normalization as a correction, and showed that binary consensus is strictly more stable than score-based ensembling.\n513\tThese findings provide practitioners with actionable diagnostics for cross-method circuit analysis.\n514\t\n515\t%======================================================================\n516\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n517\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem{bhaskar2024finding}\n520\tA.~Bhaskar, D.~Friedman, and A.~Ghandeharioun.\n521\t\\newblock Finding and editing multi-modal neurons in pre-trained transformer models.\n522\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2024.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem{conmy2023towards}\n525\tA.~Conmy, A.~N. Mavor-Parker, A.~Lynch, S.~Heimersheim, and A.~Garriga-Alonso.\n526\t\\newblock Towards automated circuit discovery for mechanistic interpretability.\n527\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2023.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem{elhage2021mathematical}\n530\tN.~Elhage, N.~Nanda, C.~Olsson, et al.\n531\t\\newblock A mathematical framework for transformer circuits.\n532\t\\newblock \\emph{Transformer Circuits Thread}, 2021.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem{finlayson2021causal}\n535\tM.~Finlayson, A.~Mueller, S.~Gehrmann, S.~Shieber, T.~Linzen, and Y.~Belinkov.\n536\t\\newblock Causal analysis of syntactic agreement mechanisms in neural language models.\n537\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACL}, 2021.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem{hanna2023how}\n540\tM.~Hanna, O.~Liu, and A.~Variengien.\n541\t\\newblock How does {GPT-2} compute greater-than over the years? {I}nterpreting the failure modes of a trained transformer.\n542\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ EMNLP}, 2023.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem{hanna2024have}\n545\tM.~Hanna, S.~Balci, and A.~Variengien.\n546\t\\newblock Have faith in faithfulness: Going beyond circuit overlap in mechanistic interpretability.\n547\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2403.17806}, 2024.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem{huo2024pie}\n550\tX.~Huo, et al.\n551\t\\newblock {PIE}: Pruning-based iterative ensembling for circuit discovery.\n552\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2604.16889}, 2024.\n553\t\n554\t\\bibitem{marks2024sparse}\n555\tS.~Marks, M.~Rager, E.~J. Michaud, Y.~Belinkov, D.~Bau, and A.~Mueller.\n556\t\\newblock Sparse feature circuits: Discovering and editing interpretable causal graphs in language models.\n557\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICML}, 2024.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem{mondorf2025from}\n560\tP.~Mondorf, S.~Ivanova, and B.~Plank.\n561\t\\newblock From overlap to insight: Comparing methods for circuit discovery.\n562\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2510.06811}, 2025.\n563\t\n564\t\\bibitem{parekh2026circus}\n565\tB.~Parekh, et al.\n566\t\\newblock {CIRCUS}: Circuit consensus via threshold diversity.\n567\t\\newblock 2026.\n568\t\n569\t\\bibitem{qwen2025qwen25}\n570\t{Qwen Team}.\n571\t\\newblock Qwen2.5 technical report.\n572\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2412.15115}, 2025.\n573\t\n574\t\\bibitem{radford2019language}\n575\tA.~Radford, J.~Wu, R.~Child, D.~Luan, D.~Amodei, and I.~Sutskever.\n576\t\\newblock Language models are unsupervised multitask learners.\n577\t\\newblock \\emph{OpenAI Blog}, 2019.\n578\t\n579\t\\bibitem{syed2023attribution}\n580\tA.~Syed, C.~Rood, and R.~Fernandes.\n581\t\\newblock Attribution patching outperforms automated circuit discovery.\n582\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2310.10348}, 2023.\n583\t\n584\t\\bibitem{wang2023interpretability}\n585\tK.~Wang, A.~Variengien, A.~Conmy, B.~Shlegeris, and J.~Steinhardt.\n586\t\\newblock Interpretability in the wild: a circuit for indirect object identification in {GPT-2} small.\n587\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2023.\n588\t\n589\t\\end{thebibliography}\n590\t\n591\t\\end{document}\n592\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"3c002331-4ab9-48a2-9adb-82f519b48b98","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:29:00.382Z"}
