{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016utY2JRVMsw6pcwjCeHYEw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{When Does Cross-Method Consensus Fail in Circuit Discovery?}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{\n16\t  Anonymous\n17\t}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tMechanistic interpretability relies on circuit discovery methods to identify computational subgraphs in neural networks, yet different methods often disagree on which edges constitute the ``true'' circuit. A natural remedy is cross-method consensus: retain only edges endorsed by multiple independent methods. We conduct a systematic empirical study of binary consensus scoring (CSS) across three algorithmic families---gradient attribution (EAP-IG), edge pruning (EP), activation patching (ACDC), and iterative feature removal (IFR)---on four tasks and four transformer models (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B). We find that CSS produces faithful, edge-efficient circuits \\emph{only} when constituent methods provide genuinely independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard similarity). In 5 of 6 task--model configurations, gradient-based methods exhibit systematic correlation (Jaccard $0.38$--$0.62$), causing CSS to degenerate. The sole positive result (IOI on GPT-2) is confounded by ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} binarization and zero-ablation evaluation bias. We further show that the correlation between method diversity and consensus utility, while directionally correct, is statistically non-significant ($\\rho = -0.54$, $p = 0.27$, $n=6$) and driven by a single influential data point. Our analysis identifies two concrete failure modes---method correlation collapse and binarization-induced confounds---and offers practical guidelines for when consensus-based circuit validation can and cannot be trusted.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\n30\tCircuit discovery in transformers aims to identify minimal subgraphs---sets of edges between attention heads and MLPs---that are sufficient to explain a model's behavior on a given task \\citep{conmy2023towards,wang2023interpretability}. Multiple methods have been proposed, including gradient-based attribution \\citep{syed2023attribution}, edge pruning \\citep{li2024edge}, and activation patching \\citep{conmy2023towards}. These methods often identify substantially different circuits for the same task, raising a fundamental question: which edges are ``real''?\n31\t\n32\tCross-method consensus offers an intuitive answer: edges identified by multiple independent methods are more likely to be causally important. We formalize this as \\textbf{Consensus Support Scoring} (CSS), where each edge receives a score $s \\in \\{0, 1/K, 2/K, \\ldots, 1\\}$ reflecting the fraction of $K$ methods that include it. The CSS$=1.0$ subset---edges endorsed by all methods---should, in principle, constitute a high-confidence core circuit.\n33\t\n34\tThis paper investigates when this intuition holds and when it breaks down. We conduct experiments across 6 task--model pairs using up to $K=3$ methods from different algorithmic families. Our central finding is negative: \\textbf{CSS fails in the majority of configurations because current circuit discovery methods are not sufficiently independent.} Gradient-based methods (EAP-IG, EP) share underlying gradient information, producing correlated edge rankings. Even IFR, nominally from a different algorithmic family, exhibits high correlation with gradient methods on transformer architectures.\n35\t\n36\tWe identify two specific confounds that inflate apparent consensus utility:\n37\t\\begin{enumerate}\n38\t    \\item \\textbf{ACDC binarization pathology}: ACDC's \\texttt{isinf} thresholding produces extremely sparse circuits (14--35 edges) that artificially lower pairwise Jaccard, making methods appear more independent than they are.\n39\t    \\item \\textbf{Zero-ablation bias}: Zero ablation disproportionately penalizes larger circuits, inflating the edge-efficiency advantage of small consensus subsets.\n40\t\\end{enumerate}\n41\t\n42\tThese findings have practical implications: researchers should verify method independence (via pairwise Jaccard) before trusting consensus-based circuit validation, and should use mean or resample ablation rather than zero ablation for faithfulness evaluation.\n43\t\n44\t\\section{Background}\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{Circuit Discovery Methods}\n47\t\n48\tWe study four circuit discovery methods spanning three algorithmic families:\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{EAP-IG (Edge Attribution Patching with Integrated Gradients).} A gradient-based method that scores each edge by the integrated gradient of the model's output with respect to that edge's activation \\citep{syed2023attribution}. Computationally efficient (single backward pass with integration).\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{EP (Edge Pruning).} An iterative pruning method that removes edges in order of a pruning criterion derived from gradient information \\citep{li2024edge}. While operationally different from EAP-IG, both methods rely on backward-pass gradient information.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{ACDC (Automatic Circuit DisCovery).} An activation patching method that iteratively tests each edge by replacing its activation with a corrupted counterfactual \\citep{conmy2023towards}. Produces binary circuits via thresholding (commonly \\texttt{isinf}).\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{IFR (Iterative Feature Removal).} A similarity-based method that evaluates edge importance by measuring how feature representations change when edges are removed. Nominally distinct from gradient methods.\n57\t\n58\t\\subsection{Consensus Support Scoring}\n59\t\n60\tGiven $K$ circuit discovery methods, each producing a binary circuit $C_k \\subseteq \\mathcal{E}$ over the edge set $\\mathcal{E}$, we define the consensus support score for edge $e$ as:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"4d0d858b-3da4-487c-bee9-f327be98ae84","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:29:18.380Z"}
