{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CBqndFAp9sdeP5oG5H6Zh","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nThe review identifies the following critical issues with the median-tier paper:\n\n**Primary concerns:**\n- The Jaccard-CSS correlation (Claim 2, the paper's centerpiece) rests on n=5 non-independent data points. Spearman p=0.037 barely crosses significance; Pearson p=0.089 does not. The ~0.45 threshold is determined by two data points within 0.012 CPR of zero.\n- CSS=1.0 wins on CPR in only 2 of 5 configurations (IOI on both models). It loses or ties on the remaining 3, making the abstract's \"comparable or superior faithfulness\" framing misleading.\n- Top-k=75 is an uncontrolled degree of freedom. SVA and Qwen IOI show sensitivity at k=50. No principled selection criterion is provided.\n- Ablation robustness analysis covers only IOI and reveals a 6x variation in CSS advantage (0.013 to 0.080 across ablation methods).\n- Different method sets across models (ACDC on GPT-2 vs IFR on Qwen) confound cross-model comparisons.\n\n**Acknowledged strengths:**\n- The ACDC isinf binarization pathology diagnosis is a genuinely useful community contribution.\n- Honest self-framing as diagnostic framework rather than claiming universal superiority.\n- Including negative results (Arithmetic) rather than cherry-picking.\n- The CIRCUS negative ablation comparison, despite scope limitations.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/median/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a92db1b4f17d12fa1 (use SendMessage with to: 'a92db1b4f17d12fa1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 110379\ntool_uses: 30\nduration_ms: 172584</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"e791153b-2d67-4d66-b6b1-10f2f13a46b2","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:30:51.532Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Evaluate as a top ML venue reviewer (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper introduces Circuit Structure Consensus (CSS), a framework that scores each edge in a transformer computational graph by the fraction of K≥3 circuit discovery methods that independently include it. The key idea: full-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) form smaller circuits with comparable faithfulness, but only when methods provide genuinely independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard).\n\n## Paper Claims\n1. CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3–5× fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45\n2. Negative correlation between method agreement (Jaccard) and CSS advantage (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037, n=5)\n3. ACDC's isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to collapse to the ACDC circuit; top-k normalization fixes this\n4. Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS)\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Models: GPT-2 Small (117M), Qwen-2.5 0.5B (500M)\n- Tasks: IOI, SVA, Greater-Than Arithmetic\n- Methods: K=3 per config. GPT-2: EAP-IG, EP, ACDC (top-k). Qwen: EAP-IG, EP, IFR (replacing ACDC)\n- Metrics: CPR (Circuit Probability Ratio), CMD (Circuit Mean Divergence), Pairwise Jaccard\n- Default: k=75, mean ablation; sensitivity over k∈{50,75,100,150} and ablation∈{zero,mean,resample}\n\n## Key Results (Table 1)\n| Task | Model | CSS=1.0 Edges/CPR | Best Single Edges/CPR | CSS Adv. | Max Jaccard |\n|------|-------|-------------------|----------------------|----------|-------------|\n| IOI | GPT-2 | 18/0.112 | 75/0.091 | +0.021 | 0.31 |\n| SVA | GPT-2 | 23/0.941 | 75/0.953 | −0.012 | 0.38 |\n| Arith | GPT-2 | 54/0.919 | 75/0.985 | −0.066 | 0.695 |\n| IOI | Qwen | 19/0.103 | 75/0.091 | +0.012 | 0.55 |\n| Arith | Qwen | 54/0.932 | 75/0.971 | −0.039 | 0.61 |\n\nCSS wins only on IOI (both models); loses on Arithmetic (both); SVA is marginal.\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context (from design document)\nThe full experiment plan included:\n- plan_004: GPT-2 SVA K=3 — DONE, partial_support\n- plan_005: GPT-2 DocQA — ABANDONED (GPT-2 too weak for factual recall, ACDC couldn't converge)\n- plan_006: Qwen IOI K=3 with IFR — DONE, mixed (IFR×EP Jaccard=0.55 unexpectedly high)\n- plan_007: Qwen Arithmetic K=3 — DONE, negative (CSS still doesn't recover)\n- plan_008: Gemma-2 2B IOI — NOT ATTEMPTED (time/GPU constraints)\n- plan_009: Llama-3.1 8B IOI — NOT ATTEMPTED\n- plan_010: Binarization k-sweep — DONE, partially_stable (k=50 sensitivity on SVA)\n- plan_011: Ablation method comparison — PARTIAL (only IOI completed)\n- plan_012: CIRCUS negative ablation — DONE, confirmed\n- plan_013: Jaccard-CSS correlation — DONE, trend observed but n=5 limits power\n\nKey context: The narrative shifted from \"CSS is empirically superior\" to \"CSS as analytical framework + binarization pathology.\" SVA provides marginal evidence; Qwen IOI has IFR-EP correlation issue reducing effective K. The Jaccard-utility Spearman p=0.037 just barely crosses significance. DocQA was dropped. Only 3 tasks on 2 small models.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Method Evaluation**\n   - Is the CSS formulation sound? Any issues with the framework definition?\n   - Is top-k normalization well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n   - Are the evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD) appropriate?\n   - Is the CSS distribution diagnostic well-defined?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**\n   - Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims?\n   - Statistical validity of the Jaccard-CSS correlation (n=5)\n   - k=75 as a degree of freedom — is the sensitivity analysis adequate?\n   - Ablation method robustness (only IOI completed) — how concerning?\n   - Using different method sets for GPT-2 vs Qwen — does this confound comparison?\n\n3. **Completeness and Coverage**\n   - Only 3 tasks, 2 small models — sufficient?\n   - DocQA abandoned, Gemma/Llama not attempted — impact?\n   - IFR-EP correlation reducing effective K on Qwen — adequately addressed?\n   - CIRCUS comparison uses zero ablation (not mean) and only IOI — fair comparison?\n\n4. **Specific Technical Issues**\n   - Table 1 shows Arith GPT-2 best single CPR=0.985 but context says EAP-IG=0.971 for Qwen Arith; check consistency\n   - SVA CSS=1.0 CPR=0.941 is actually *between* EP (0.937) and EAP-IG (0.953) — the \"comparable\" claim is fair but CSS doesn't win\n   - The Jaccard threshold ~0.45 is estimated from 5 points with 2 on each side of the boundary — how reliable?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n7. **Score and Confidence**\n   - Overall score (1-6)\n   - Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n   - Brief justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper explicitly frames CSS as a diagnostic framework, not claiming universal superiority. Evaluate whether the evidence supports even this more modest framing.\n","agentId":"a92db1b4f17d12fa1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nThe review identifies the following critical issues with the median-tier paper:\n\n**Primary concerns:**\n- The Jaccard-CSS correlation (Claim 2, the paper's centerpiece) rests on n=5 non-independent data points. Spearman p=0.037 barely crosses significance; Pearson p=0.089 does not. The ~0.45 threshold is determined by two data points within 0.012 CPR of zero.\n- CSS=1.0 wins on CPR in only 2 of 5 configurations (IOI on both models). It loses or ties on the remaining 3, making the abstract's \"comparable or superior faithfulness\" framing misleading.\n- Top-k=75 is an uncontrolled degree of freedom. SVA and Qwen IOI show sensitivity at k=50. No principled selection criterion is provided.\n- Ablation robustness analysis covers only IOI and reveals a 6x variation in CSS advantage (0.013 to 0.080 across ablation methods).\n- Different method sets across models (ACDC on GPT-2 vs IFR on Qwen) confound cross-model comparisons.\n\n**Acknowledged strengths:**\n- The ACDC isinf binarization pathology diagnosis is a genuinely useful community contribution.\n- Honest self-framing as diagnostic framework rather than claiming universal superiority.\n- Including negative results (Arithmetic) rather than cherry-picking.\n- The CIRCUS negative ablation comparison, despite scope limitations.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T054654/median/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":172584,"totalTokens":110379,"totalToolUseCount":30,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3903,"cache_read_input_tokens":106030,"output_tokens":445,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3903},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":445,"cache_read_input_tokens":106030,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3903,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3903,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":14,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
