{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ettpxm3wsU6RjATWuuXeUc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize the R2 review focusing on novelty and related work.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**How novel is CSS?**\n\nCSS is, at its core, majority voting on binary set membership. The formula CSS(e) = (1/K) * sum(1[e in S_i]) is the inclusion frequency used in feature selection stability analysis since at least Saeys et al. (2007) and formalized by Nogueira & Brown (JMLR 2016) as the \"stability index.\" The mechanistic interpretability community has not previously applied this to circuit edges across algorithmically diverse methods, so there is domain-specific novelty, but the mathematical framework is well-established outside MI. The paper does not acknowledge this connection at all.\n\n**Is \"consensus = intersection\" obvious?**\n\nThe idea that intersecting outputs from multiple classifiers/selectors yields higher-confidence subsets is a foundational principle in ensemble methods, dating back to voting classifiers (Condorcet, 1785; Breiman, 1996). Applied to circuit discovery, it is natural enough that Mondorf et al. (2025) already implemented union, intersection, and mean aggregation of circuit outputs. The formalization does add value in one specific way: connecting Jaccard diversity to consensus utility. This is the paper's genuinely interesting insight. But the formalization of CSS itself (the equation) adds minimal value over \"intersect the edge sets.\"\n\n**Incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. (2025)**\n\nMondorf et al. already performed cross-method aggregation (including intersection = CSS=1.0) and found that mean score averaging outperforms intersection. Their mean ensemble achieves CPR=1.83 on MIB, dramatically exceeding any CSS result reported here (max CPR=0.14 on IOI). The paper's claimed differentiation --- binary consensus vs. continuous averaging, and cross-family vs. within-gradient-family --- is valid but the paper never runs a head-to-head comparison. The absence of this comparison is a significant gap: it is entirely possible that Mondorf et al.'s approach, extended to cross-family methods, would dominate CSS.\n\n**Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?**\n\nYes, this is a genuine and useful contribution to the community. The documentation that ACDC's isinf threshold causes CSS to degenerate to the ACDC circuit is a concrete, actionable finding. Any researcher comparing ACDC outputs with gradient-based circuits should be aware of this size mismatch. The top-k normalization fix is simple but necessary. This finding stands on its own regardless of whether CSS itself is adopted.\n\n**Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?**\n\nThe directional insight (consensus is more useful when methods disagree) is intuitive and arguably obvious. The quantification via Spearman correlation is the paper's attempt to make this actionable. However, the median-tier paper has n=5 data points (rho=-0.90, p=0.037), while the best-tier paper inflates to n=6 using simulated data (rho=0.94, p=0.005). In either case, the sample is too small to establish a reliable threshold. The two-cluster structure of the data (low-Jaccard/positive-Delta vs. high-Jaccard/negative-Delta) means a simple binary rule (\"if Jaccard < 0.5, use consensus\") fits equally well as a continuous correlation --- the Spearman test is not the right tool here.\n\n**Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?**\n\nThe diagnostic framing is intellectually honest but thin. The paper offers three diagnostic contributions: (1) CSS score distribution reveals task structure; (2) Jaccard predicts CSS utility; (3) ACDC binarization pathology detection. Of these, only (3) is fully validated. Item (1) is demonstrated on 2-3 tasks with no formal analysis of what \"dispersed\" vs. \"concentrated\" distributions imply. Item (2) rests on 5 data points. For EMNLP 2026 Main, this is insufficient scope.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Missing references (critical gaps):**\n\n- **Feature selection stability literature**: Saeys et al. (2007, Bioinformatics); Nogueira & Brown (2016, JMLR). CSS is mathematically identical to the feature selection stability index. This omission makes the novelty appear inflated.\n- **Ensemble methods in ML**: Breiman (1996, bagging); Dietterich (2000, ensemble methods). The conditions under which ensemble agreement is informative (diversity, error independence) are well-studied.\n- **Miller et al. (COLM 2024)**: \"Transformer Circuit Faithfulness Metrics are not Robust\" --- directly motivates CSS and is absent from the paper despite being present in the project's own literature survey.\n- **Meloux et al. (2025)**: Intra-method instability of EAP-IG (Jaccard=0.561 within a single method under bootstrap) --- critical context for CSS's claim that cross-method consensus adds signal.\n- **Sharkey et al. (2025)**: \"Open Problems in Mechanistic Interpretability\" --- explicitly recommends reporting explanation ensembles. CSS operationalizes this recommendation and should cite it.\n- **MIB benchmark (ICML 2025)**: Standardized evaluation that contextualizes method performance differences.\n- **Recent discovery methods**: CD-T (Hsu et al., ICLR 2025), GIM (Edin et al., 2025), HAP (Bhaskar et al., NeurIPS 2025), Sparse Feature Circuits (Marks et al., ICLR 2025 Oral) --- all relevant as potential additional methods for CSS or as positioning context.\n- **Franco et al. (2026)**: Prompt-specific circuits --- challenges CSS to demonstrate stability across prompts.\n- **Circuit non-uniqueness**: \"Everything Everywhere All at Once\" (ICLR 2025) --- if circuits are fundamentally non-unique, the theoretical basis for CSS needs stronger justification.\n\nThe median-tier paper has 14 references; the best-tier has 8. Both are severely underreferenced for a paper positioning itself within a rapidly growing literature. A well-positioned paper in this space needs 30-40 references minimum.\n\n**Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair?**\n\n- **CIRCUS**: The positioning is conceptually fair (within-method threshold diversity vs. cross-method algorithmic diversity is a genuine distinction). However, the empirical comparison uses a known-broken implementation (1D percentile sweep producing nested circuits, violating CIRCUS's non-nesting constraint; three failed repair attempts documented in D013, D016, D017, D020, D021). Table 9 (median) / Table showing CIRCUS with negative CPR likely reflects implementation bugs, not a fundamental limitation of within-method stability. This comparison should be either fixed or removed.\n\n- **Mondorf et al.**: The positioning is incomplete. The paper claims CSS's binary consensus avoids score calibration, which is true. But it never acknowledges that Mondorf et al.'s continuous averaging achieves dramatically higher absolute faithfulness. A fair comparison would acknowledge this and argue for CSS on other grounds (interpretability of binary membership, no calibration needed) rather than implying CSS is superior.\n\n**Should there be discussion of other aggregation strategies?**\n\nYes. The paper only considers full intersection (CSS=1.0) and majority (CSS >= 2/3). The literature on combining classifier outputs includes weighted voting, stacking, Bayesian model combination, and rank aggregation. At minimum, the paper should discuss why simple intersection was chosen over these alternatives and whether weighted consensus (giving more weight to methods with higher standalone faithfulness) could improve results.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claim-Evidence Alignment\n\n**Claim 1: CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3-5x fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45.**\n\n- Evidence: In the median tier (closest to real data), CSS advantage is positive only on IOI GPT-2 (+0.021) and IOI Qwen (+0.012). SVA is marginal (-0.012). Arithmetic is negative on both models.\n- Assessment: The claim is weakly supported. \"Comparable faithfulness\" is a stretch when the advantage is +0.021 CPR (2.1 percentage points) without confidence intervals. The edge-efficiency claim is valid but trivially follows from intersection being smaller than any constituent set. The Jaccard threshold of 0.45 is estimated from 5 points and has no confidence interval.\n- Calibration: Overclaimed. \"Comparable\" suggests rough equivalence; the actual numbers show CSS is sometimes better by tiny margins (IOI) and sometimes worse (SVA, Arithmetic). The 3-5x edge reduction is real but its practical significance is unclear when absolute CPR values are so low (0.103-0.112 on IOI).\n\n**Claim 2: Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman rho=-0.90, p=0.037, n=5).**\n\n- Evidence: 5 data points that cluster into two groups. The correlation is driven by task structure (localized vs. distributed), not by a smooth relationship.\n- Assessment: The p-value (0.037) barely crosses 0.05 at n=5. A single data point swap could invalidate it. The mechanistic intuition is sound, but the statistical claim overstates the evidence. The best-tier paper inflates to n=6 with simulated data and reports rho=0.94, p=0.005, which is misleading.\n- Calibration: The median tier is honestly calibrated (acknowledges n=5 limitation). The best tier overclaims.\n\n**Claim 3: ACDC isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this.**\n\n- Evidence: Fully demonstrated on both IOI and Arithmetic with clear numerical evidence.\n- Assessment: Strong. This is the most convincingly supported claim.\n- Calibration: Appropriately calibrated.\n\n**Claim 4: Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS).**\n\n- Evidence: CIRCUS produces negative CPR on IOI under zero ablation. CSS produces positive CPR.\n- Assessment: The comparison is unreliable. The CIRCUS implementation is documented as broken (1D nested thresholds, scope mismatch). The comparison uses only IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation). Zero ablation is known to be the most favorable for small circuits (Miller et al., COLM 2024). This claim cannot be supported without a corrected CIRCUS implementation.\n- Calibration: Overclaimed given implementation issues.\n\n**Is \"diagnostic framework\" a strength or weakness?**\n\nIt is both. It is honest and avoids overclaiming --- a genuine strength. But it also means the paper's actionable contribution is limited: \"compute Jaccard, then decide\" is a one-sentence guideline. If the paper is positioned as diagnostic, it needs deeper diagnostic analysis: what does the CSS distribution tell us about circuit structure? Can CSS identify method-specific artifacts? What do off-diagonal edges reveal? The current analysis is too shallow for the framing.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Framing and Positioning\n\n**Is the paper well-positioned within MI literature?**\n\nNo. The related work section is too thin (8-14 references depending on tier) and misses critical connections to feature selection stability, ensemble learning, and several recent MI papers. The paper reads as if the authors surveyed only the immediate circuit discovery literature without looking at adjacent fields where identical mathematical frameworks exist.\n\n**Does the paper make clear what practitioners should do with CSS?**\n\nThe median tier is better here: it provides a concrete two-step workflow (compute Jaccard, then decide). But the practical value is limited: running K >= 3 discovery methods is expensive (ACDC alone takes hours on GPT-2 Small), and the paper doesn't discuss computational cost. A practitioner might reasonably ask: \"Why not just use the best single method?\"\n\n**Is the narrative coherent?**\n\nThe median tier tells a clearer story than the best tier. It honestly frames CSS as a diagnostic tool, presents both positive and negative results, and avoids overclaiming. The best tier inflates results with simulated data and presents a more aggressive narrative that the evidence doesn't support.\n\n**Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery?**\n\nMarginally. The binarization pathology finding is useful and actionable. The general principle \"check method agreement before trusting consensus\" is sound but obvious. The specific Jaccard threshold is too weakly supported to be actionable. Overall, this is a useful empirical observation paper but not one that changes practice.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Strengths\n\n- Honest treatment of negative results: Arithmetic as a principled failure case is more scientifically valuable than a universal superiority claim\n- The ACDC isinf binarization pathology is a concrete, actionable finding that the community should know about\n- The research question is well-motivated: different methods disagree, and understanding this disagreement is important\n- Binary consensus avoids the score calibration problem inherent in continuous averaging across methods with incomparable scales\n- The conditional framing (CSS helps when methods disagree) is intellectually honest\n- The median-tier paper's limitations section is thorough and forthcoming\n- Top-k normalization as a preprocessing step for fair cross-method comparison is a useful methodological contribution\n\n### 6. Weaknesses\n\n- **CSS is mathematically identical to the feature selection stability index** (Nogueira & Brown 2016), a connection entirely unacknowledged. This is the single largest novelty gap.\n- **Only 8-14 references** in a literature that has 50+ relevant papers from 2024-2026 alone. The paper is severely underreferenced.\n- **No head-to-head comparison with Mondorf et al.'s continuous averaging**, despite this being the most direct competitor. Mondorf et al. report CPR=1.83 vs. CSS's max CPR=0.14 --- a 13x gap that the paper does not address.\n- **CIRCUS comparison uses a known-broken implementation** (documented across decisions D013, D016, D017, D020, D021). This invalidates one of the four claims.\n- **Absolute CPR values are very low** (0.10-0.11 on IOI) compared to published results for ACDC/EP at comparable sparsities. The paper does not explain or acknowledge this.\n- **No size-controlled baselines**: CSS=1.0 has 7-19 edges vs. 75 for single methods. The comparison never controls for circuit size (e.g., comparing CSS=1.0 at N edges against the top-N edges of each single method).\n- **No confidence intervals** on any CPR difference. A +0.021 advantage could easily be within noise.\n- **n=5 for the central correlation claim** with a two-cluster structure that a step function fits equally well\n- **IFR is underspecified**: no pseudocode, no standalone evaluation, known granularity mismatch (layer-level vs. edge-level)\n- **Best-tier paper uses simulated/projected data** for the majority of reported results (SVA, Factual Recall, all cross-model results), while presenting them as experimental findings\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Feature selection stability**: How does CSS differ from the feature selection stability index (Nogueira & Brown, JMLR 2016), which computes the same quantity (fraction of selectors that include a feature) in a well-studied setting? If the only difference is the domain (circuit edges vs. features), what does the MI-specific application contribute beyond domain transfer?\n\n2. **Size-controlled comparison**: What is the CPR of the top-N edges from EAP-IG alone, where N = |CSS=1.0 circuit|? If EAP-IG's top-14 edges on IOI also achieve CPR >= 0.10, then CSS's advantage is entirely attributable to circuit size, not consensus.\n\n3. **Mondorf et al. head-to-head**: Have you compared CSS against continuous score averaging (mean ensemble from Mondorf et al.) on the same tasks? If their approach achieves higher absolute CPR, what is the argument for using binary consensus instead?\n\n4. **Computational cost**: What is the total wall-clock time for running K=3 methods (EAP-IG + EP + ACDC) on GPT-2 Small? On Qwen-2.5 0.5B? Is the recommendation to \"run 3+ diverse methods\" realistic for practitioners working with limited compute?\n\n5. **CIRCUS implementation**: Was the CIRCUS implementation's known non-nesting violation (documented in your own D013 decision log) corrected before generating the comparison results? If not, the claim that \"binary consensus is more stable than score-based ensembling\" is not supported.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a genuinely important question (when do circuit discovery methods agree, and does agreement imply quality?) and provides one solid contribution (the ACDC binarization pathology finding). The diagnostic framing is honest and the negative results are scientifically valuable. However, the novelty is significantly undermined by the unacknowledged connection to feature selection stability (identical mathematical framework), the missing head-to-head with Mondorf et al., and the broken CIRCUS comparison. The central statistical claim (Jaccard-utility correlation) rests on n=5 data points. The related work coverage is inadequate for a venue like EMNLP Main. With a thorough related work section acknowledging the feature selection stability literature, a corrected CIRCUS comparison (or its removal), a head-to-head with Mondorf et al., size-controlled baselines, and 3-5 additional task-model configurations for the correlation analysis, this could become a solid Findings paper or a weak Main paper. In its current state, it does not clear the bar for a top venue.\n\nI score this a 3 rather than a 2 because: (a) the research question is timely and well-motivated, (b) the binarization pathology is a genuine contribution, (c) the conditional framing (CSS helps only when methods disagree) is more scientifically mature than a universal claim would be, and (d) the median-tier paper's self-awareness in its limitations section shows scholarly rigor. A 2 would mean the paper has fundamental conceptual flaws; the flaws here are primarily about scope, positioning, and incomplete execution rather than conceptual unsoundness."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a32ebe74fd5eaebf4 (use SendMessage with to: 'a32ebe74fd5eaebf4' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85758\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 174727</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da6c2831-13c3-4ab7-a8ff-27a4cd9a02b2","uuid":"e96b84a8-8d4b-4a08-a239-5a98d3b6d7a9","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:31:20.071Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Evaluate as a top ML venue reviewer (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper introduces Circuit Structure Consensus (CSS), a framework that scores each edge in a transformer computational graph by the fraction of K≥3 circuit discovery methods that independently include it. Full-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) form compact circuits. The paper argues CSS is most valuable as a diagnostic tool: revealing when cross-method agreement is meaningful vs. artifactual.\n\n## Paper Claims\n1. CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3–5× fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45\n2. Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037, n=5)\n3. ACDC's isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this\n4. Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS)\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n- **Circuit discovery**: Cites activation patching [Conmy 2023, Bhaskar 2024], gradient-based [Syed 2023, Marks 2024], representation similarity [Hanna 2024]. Notes distinct inductive biases.\n- **Cross-method comparison**: Cites Mondorf et al. [2025] who compared methods and proposed score averaging within gradient family. Paper differs by using binary consensus and cross-family methods. Also cites PIE [Huo 2024] for iterative pruning ensembles within single paradigm.\n- **Circuit ensembling**: Cites CIRCUS [Parekh 2026] for threshold diversity within single method. Paper claims CSS's cross-method approach succeeds where CIRCUS's within-method approach fails.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n| Task | Model | CSS=1.0 Edges/CPR | Best Single Edges/CPR | CSS Adv. | Max Jaccard |\n|------|-------|-------------------|----------------------|----------|-------------|\n| IOI | GPT-2 | 18/0.112 | 75/0.091 | +0.021 | 0.31 |\n| SVA | GPT-2 | 23/0.941 | 75/0.953 | −0.012 | 0.38 |\n| Arith | GPT-2 | 54/0.919 | 75/0.985 | −0.066 | 0.695 |\n| IOI | Qwen | 19/0.103 | 75/0.091 | +0.012 | 0.55 |\n| Arith | Qwen | 54/0.932 | 75/0.971 | −0.039 | 0.61 |\n\nCSS advantage is positive only on IOI (both models). Negative on Arithmetic. SVA is marginal (−0.012). The Jaccard-utility correlation has n=5 data points.\n\n## Additional Context\n- The paper positions CSS as a **diagnostic framework** rather than claiming it's a superior circuit extraction method\n- The CIRCUS comparison is only on IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation)\n- ACDC binarization pathology is a useful community finding\n- IFR-EP Jaccard=0.55 on Qwen IOI suggests \"different algorithmic family ≠ measurement independence\"\n- The paper covers 3 tasks × 2 models but originally planned more (DocQA abandoned, Gemma-2/Llama not attempted)\n- The paper has 14 references\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is the CSS framework? Is it essentially majority voting on binary circuit membership?\n   - Is the \"consensus = intersection\" idea obvious or does the formalization add value?\n   - What is the incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. [2025] who also compared methods?\n   - Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?\n   - Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?\n   - Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**\n   - Are there missing references? Consider: ensemble methods in ML more broadly, voting classifiers, Bayesian model combination, other circuit discovery papers\n   - Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair and accurate?\n   - Should there be discussion of: faithfulness metrics beyond CPR/CMD, other ways to combine circuit outputs (union, weighted), existing diagnostics in mechanistic interpretability?\n   - The paper has only 14 references — is this adequate for the breadth of related work?\n\n3. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**\n   - For each of the 4 claims, assess: Is the evidence sufficient? Is the claim appropriately calibrated?\n   - Is \"diagnostic framework\" framing a strength (honest/modest) or weakness (low ambition)?\n   - Does the paper deliver on its stated contributions?\n\n4. **Framing and Positioning**\n   - Is the paper well-positioned within the mechanistic interpretability literature?\n   - Does the paper make clear what practitioners should actually do with CSS?\n   - Is the narrative coherent: does the paper tell a clear story from motivation to results?\n   - Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery in practice?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n7. **Score and Confidence**\n   - Overall score (1-6)\n   - Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n   - Brief justification\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider whether the diagnostic/analytical framing is genuinely useful to the mechanistic interpretability community, even if the method itself is simple.\n","agentId":"a32ebe74fd5eaebf4","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize the R2 review focusing on novelty and related work.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** \"When Do Circuit Discovery Methods Agree? Consensus Scoring as a Diagnostic Framework for Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**How novel is CSS?**\n\nCSS is, at its core, majority voting on binary set membership. The formula CSS(e) = (1/K) * sum(1[e in S_i]) is the inclusion frequency used in feature selection stability analysis since at least Saeys et al. (2007) and formalized by Nogueira & Brown (JMLR 2016) as the \"stability index.\" The mechanistic interpretability community has not previously applied this to circuit edges across algorithmically diverse methods, so there is domain-specific novelty, but the mathematical framework is well-established outside MI. The paper does not acknowledge this connection at all.\n\n**Is \"consensus = intersection\" obvious?**\n\nThe idea that intersecting outputs from multiple classifiers/selectors yields higher-confidence subsets is a foundational principle in ensemble methods, dating back to voting classifiers (Condorcet, 1785; Breiman, 1996). Applied to circuit discovery, it is natural enough that Mondorf et al. (2025) already implemented union, intersection, and mean aggregation of circuit outputs. The formalization does add value in one specific way: connecting Jaccard diversity to consensus utility. This is the paper's genuinely interesting insight. But the formalization of CSS itself (the equation) adds minimal value over \"intersect the edge sets.\"\n\n**Incremental contribution over Mondorf et al. (2025)**\n\nMondorf et al. already performed cross-method aggregation (including intersection = CSS=1.0) and found that mean score averaging outperforms intersection. Their mean ensemble achieves CPR=1.83 on MIB, dramatically exceeding any CSS result reported here (max CPR=0.14 on IOI). The paper's claimed differentiation --- binary consensus vs. continuous averaging, and cross-family vs. within-gradient-family --- is valid but the paper never runs a head-to-head comparison. The absence of this comparison is a significant gap: it is entirely possible that Mondorf et al.'s approach, extended to cross-family methods, would dominate CSS.\n\n**Is the binarization pathology finding novel and significant?**\n\nYes, this is a genuine and useful contribution to the community. The documentation that ACDC's isinf threshold causes CSS to degenerate to the ACDC circuit is a concrete, actionable finding. Any researcher comparing ACDC outputs with gradient-based circuits should be aware of this size mismatch. The top-k normalization fix is simple but necessary. This finding stands on its own regardless of whether CSS itself is adopted.\n\n**Is the Jaccard-as-predictor insight genuinely new?**\n\nThe directional insight (consensus is more useful when methods disagree) is intuitive and arguably obvious. The quantification via Spearman correlation is the paper's attempt to make this actionable. However, the median-tier paper has n=5 data points (rho=-0.90, p=0.037), while the best-tier paper inflates to n=6 using simulated data (rho=0.94, p=0.005). In either case, the sample is too small to establish a reliable threshold. The two-cluster structure of the data (low-Jaccard/positive-Delta vs. high-Jaccard/negative-Delta) means a simple binary rule (\"if Jaccard < 0.5, use consensus\") fits equally well as a continuous correlation --- the Spearman test is not the right tool here.\n\n**Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?**\n\nThe diagnostic framing is intellectually honest but thin. The paper offers three diagnostic contributions: (1) CSS score distribution reveals task structure; (2) Jaccard predicts CSS utility; (3) ACDC binarization pathology detection. Of these, only (3) is fully validated. Item (1) is demonstrated on 2-3 tasks with no formal analysis of what \"dispersed\" vs. \"concentrated\" distributions imply. Item (2) rests on 5 data points. For EMNLP 2026 Main, this is insufficient scope.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Missing references (critical gaps):**\n\n- **Feature selection stability literature**: Saeys et al. (2007, Bioinformatics); Nogueira & Brown (2016, JMLR). CSS is mathematically identical to the feature selection stability index. This omission makes the novelty appear inflated.\n- **Ensemble methods in ML**: Breiman (1996, bagging); Dietterich (2000, ensemble methods). The conditions under which ensemble agreement is informative (diversity, error independence) are well-studied.\n- **Miller et al. (COLM 2024)**: \"Transformer Circuit Faithfulness Metrics are not Robust\" --- directly motivates CSS and is absent from the paper despite being present in the project's own literature survey.\n- **Meloux et al. (2025)**: Intra-method instability of EAP-IG (Jaccard=0.561 within a single method under bootstrap) --- critical context for CSS's claim that cross-method consensus adds signal.\n- **Sharkey et al. (2025)**: \"Open Problems in Mechanistic Interpretability\" --- explicitly recommends reporting explanation ensembles. CSS operationalizes this recommendation and should cite it.\n- **MIB benchmark (ICML 2025)**: Standardized evaluation that contextualizes method performance differences.\n- **Recent discovery methods**: CD-T (Hsu et al., ICLR 2025), GIM (Edin et al., 2025), HAP (Bhaskar et al., NeurIPS 2025), Sparse Feature Circuits (Marks et al., ICLR 2025 Oral) --- all relevant as potential additional methods for CSS or as positioning context.\n- **Franco et al. (2026)**: Prompt-specific circuits --- challenges CSS to demonstrate stability across prompts.\n- **Circuit non-uniqueness**: \"Everything Everywhere All at Once\" (ICLR 2025) --- if circuits are fundamentally non-unique, the theoretical basis for CSS needs stronger justification.\n\nThe median-tier paper has 14 references; the best-tier has 8. Both are severely underreferenced for a paper positioning itself within a rapidly growing literature. A well-positioned paper in this space needs 30-40 references minimum.\n\n**Is the positioning against Mondorf et al. and CIRCUS fair?**\n\n- **CIRCUS**: The positioning is conceptually fair (within-method threshold diversity vs. cross-method algorithmic diversity is a genuine distinction). However, the empirical comparison uses a known-broken implementation (1D percentile sweep producing nested circuits, violating CIRCUS's non-nesting constraint; three failed repair attempts documented in D013, D016, D017, D020, D021). Table 9 (median) / Table showing CIRCUS with negative CPR likely reflects implementation bugs, not a fundamental limitation of within-method stability. This comparison should be either fixed or removed.\n\n- **Mondorf et al.**: The positioning is incomplete. The paper claims CSS's binary consensus avoids score calibration, which is true. But it never acknowledges that Mondorf et al.'s continuous averaging achieves dramatically higher absolute faithfulness. A fair comparison would acknowledge this and argue for CSS on other grounds (interpretability of binary membership, no calibration needed) rather than implying CSS is superior.\n\n**Should there be discussion of other aggregation strategies?**\n\nYes. The paper only considers full intersection (CSS=1.0) and majority (CSS >= 2/3). The literature on combining classifier outputs includes weighted voting, stacking, Bayesian model combination, and rank aggregation. At minimum, the paper should discuss why simple intersection was chosen over these alternatives and whether weighted consensus (giving more weight to methods with higher standalone faithfulness) could improve results.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claim-Evidence Alignment\n\n**Claim 1: CSS=1.0 circuits achieve comparable faithfulness using 3-5x fewer edges, but only when max pairwise Jaccard < ~0.45.**\n\n- Evidence: In the median tier (closest to real data), CSS advantage is positive only on IOI GPT-2 (+0.021) and IOI Qwen (+0.012). SVA is marginal (-0.012). Arithmetic is negative on both models.\n- Assessment: The claim is weakly supported. \"Comparable faithfulness\" is a stretch when the advantage is +0.021 CPR (2.1 percentage points) without confidence intervals. The edge-efficiency claim is valid but trivially follows from intersection being smaller than any constituent set. The Jaccard threshold of 0.45 is estimated from 5 points and has no confidence interval.\n- Calibration: Overclaimed. \"Comparable\" suggests rough equivalence; the actual numbers show CSS is sometimes better by tiny margins (IOI) and sometimes worse (SVA, Arithmetic). The 3-5x edge reduction is real but its practical significance is unclear when absolute CPR values are so low (0.103-0.112 on IOI).\n\n**Claim 2: Negative correlation between Jaccard and CSS advantage (Spearman rho=-0.90, p=0.037, n=5).**\n\n- Evidence: 5 data points that cluster into two groups. The correlation is driven by task structure (localized vs. distributed), not by a smooth relationship.\n- Assessment: The p-value (0.037) barely crosses 0.05 at n=5. A single data point swap could invalidate it. The mechanistic intuition is sound, but the statistical claim overstates the evidence. The best-tier paper inflates to n=6 with simulated data and reports rho=0.94, p=0.005, which is misleading.\n- Calibration: The median tier is honestly calibrated (acknowledges n=5 limitation). The best tier overclaims.\n\n**Claim 3: ACDC isinf binarization causes CSS=1.0 to degenerate to ACDC circuit; top-k fixes this.**\n\n- Evidence: Fully demonstrated on both IOI and Arithmetic with clear numerical evidence.\n- Assessment: Strong. This is the most convincingly supported claim.\n- Calibration: Appropriately calibrated.\n\n**Claim 4: Binary consensus (CSS) is more stable than score-based ensembling (CIRCUS).**\n\n- Evidence: CIRCUS produces negative CPR on IOI under zero ablation. CSS produces positive CPR.\n- Assessment: The comparison is unreliable. The CIRCUS implementation is documented as broken (1D nested thresholds, scope mismatch). The comparison uses only IOI with zero ablation (not the default mean ablation). Zero ablation is known to be the most favorable for small circuits (Miller et al., COLM 2024). This claim cannot be supported without a corrected CIRCUS implementation.\n- Calibration: Overclaimed given implementation issues.\n\n**Is \"diagnostic framework\" a strength or weakness?**\n\nIt is both. It is honest and avoids overclaiming --- a genuine strength. But it also means the paper's actionable contribution is limited: \"compute Jaccard, then decide\" is a one-sentence guideline. If the paper is positioned as diagnostic, it needs deeper diagnostic analysis: what does the CSS distribution tell us about circuit structure? Can CSS identify method-specific artifacts? What do off-diagonal edges reveal? The current analysis is too shallow for the framing.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Framing and Positioning\n\n**Is the paper well-positioned within MI literature?**\n\nNo. The related work section is too thin (8-14 references depending on tier) and misses critical connections to feature selection stability, ensemble learning, and several recent MI papers. The paper reads as if the authors surveyed only the immediate circuit discovery literature without looking at adjacent fields where identical mathematical frameworks exist.\n\n**Does the paper make clear what practitioners should do with CSS?**\n\nThe median tier is better here: it provides a concrete two-step workflow (compute Jaccard, then decide). But the practical value is limited: running K >= 3 discovery methods is expensive (ACDC alone takes hours on GPT-2 Small), and the paper doesn't discuss computational cost. A practitioner might reasonably ask: \"Why not just use the best single method?\"\n\n**Is the narrative coherent?**\n\nThe median tier tells a clearer story than the best tier. It honestly frames CSS as a diagnostic tool, presents both positive and negative results, and avoids overclaiming. The best tier inflates results with simulated data and presents a more aggressive narrative that the evidence doesn't support.\n\n**Would this paper change how people do circuit discovery?**\n\nMarginally. The binarization pathology finding is useful and actionable. The general principle \"check method agreement before trusting consensus\" is sound but obvious. The specific Jaccard threshold is too weakly supported to be actionable. Overall, this is a useful empirical observation paper but not one that changes practice.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Strengths\n\n- Honest treatment of negative results: Arithmetic as a principled failure case is more scientifically valuable than a universal superiority claim\n- The ACDC isinf binarization pathology is a concrete, actionable finding that the community should know about\n- The research question is well-motivated: different methods disagree, and understanding this disagreement is important\n- Binary consensus avoids the score calibration problem inherent in continuous averaging across methods with incomparable scales\n- The conditional framing (CSS helps when methods disagree) is intellectually honest\n- The median-tier paper's limitations section is thorough and forthcoming\n- Top-k normalization as a preprocessing step for fair cross-method comparison is a useful methodological contribution\n\n### 6. Weaknesses\n\n- **CSS is mathematically identical to the feature selection stability index** (Nogueira & Brown 2016), a connection entirely unacknowledged. This is the single largest novelty gap.\n- **Only 8-14 references** in a literature that has 50+ relevant papers from 2024-2026 alone. The paper is severely underreferenced.\n- **No head-to-head comparison with Mondorf et al.'s continuous averaging**, despite this being the most direct competitor. Mondorf et al. report CPR=1.83 vs. CSS's max CPR=0.14 --- a 13x gap that the paper does not address.\n- **CIRCUS comparison uses a known-broken implementation** (documented across decisions D013, D016, D017, D020, D021). This invalidates one of the four claims.\n- **Absolute CPR values are very low** (0.10-0.11 on IOI) compared to published results for ACDC/EP at comparable sparsities. The paper does not explain or acknowledge this.\n- **No size-controlled baselines**: CSS=1.0 has 7-19 edges vs. 75 for single methods. The comparison never controls for circuit size (e.g., comparing CSS=1.0 at N edges against the top-N edges of each single method).\n- **No confidence intervals** on any CPR difference. A +0.021 advantage could easily be within noise.\n- **n=5 for the central correlation claim** with a two-cluster structure that a step function fits equally well\n- **IFR is underspecified**: no pseudocode, no standalone evaluation, known granularity mismatch (layer-level vs. edge-level)\n- **Best-tier paper uses simulated/projected data** for the majority of reported results (SVA, Factual Recall, all cross-model results), while presenting them as experimental findings\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Feature selection stability**: How does CSS differ from the feature selection stability index (Nogueira & Brown, JMLR 2016), which computes the same quantity (fraction of selectors that include a feature) in a well-studied setting? If the only difference is the domain (circuit edges vs. features), what does the MI-specific application contribute beyond domain transfer?\n\n2. **Size-controlled comparison**: What is the CPR of the top-N edges from EAP-IG alone, where N = |CSS=1.0 circuit|? If EAP-IG's top-14 edges on IOI also achieve CPR >= 0.10, then CSS's advantage is entirely attributable to circuit size, not consensus.\n\n3. **Mondorf et al. head-to-head**: Have you compared CSS against continuous score averaging (mean ensemble from Mondorf et al.) on the same tasks? If their approach achieves higher absolute CPR, what is the argument for using binary consensus instead?\n\n4. **Computational cost**: What is the total wall-clock time for running K=3 methods (EAP-IG + EP + ACDC) on GPT-2 Small? On Qwen-2.5 0.5B? Is the recommendation to \"run 3+ diverse methods\" realistic for practitioners working with limited compute?\n\n5. **CIRCUS implementation**: Was the CIRCUS implementation's known non-nesting violation (documented in your own D013 decision log) corrected before generating the comparison results? If not, the claim that \"binary consensus is more stable than score-based ensembling\" is not supported.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score and Confidence\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a genuinely important question (when do circuit discovery methods agree, and does agreement imply quality?) and provides one solid contribution (the ACDC binarization pathology finding). The diagnostic framing is honest and the negative results are scientifically valuable. However, the novelty is significantly undermined by the unacknowledged connection to feature selection stability (identical mathematical framework), the missing head-to-head with Mondorf et al., and the broken CIRCUS comparison. The central statistical claim (Jaccard-utility correlation) rests on n=5 data points. The related work coverage is inadequate for a venue like EMNLP Main. With a thorough related work section acknowledging the feature selection stability literature, a corrected CIRCUS comparison (or its removal), a head-to-head with Mondorf et al., size-controlled baselines, and 3-5 additional task-model configurations for the correlation analysis, this could become a solid Findings paper or a weak Main paper. In its current state, it does not clear the bar for a top venue.\n\nI score this a 3 rather than a 2 because: (a) the research question is timely and well-motivated, (b) the binarization pathology is a genuine contribution, (c) the conditional framing (CSS helps only when methods disagree) is more scientifically mature than a universal claim would be, and (d) the median-tier paper's self-awareness in its limitations section shows scholarly rigor. A 2 would mean the paper has fundamental conceptual flaws; the flaws here are primarily about scope, positioning, and incomplete execution rather than conceptual unsoundness."}],"totalDurationMs":174727,"totalTokens":85758,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":80789,"output_tokens":4443,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":523},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4443,"cache_read_input_tokens":80789,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":523,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ML8vW8q3tjWRQXmZMmThmN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Let me now summarize the key findings from each and proceed with the roundtable process.\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): Score 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5**\nKey issues:\n- Jaccard-CSS correlation rests on n=5 non-independent data points\n- CSS wins only 2/5 configs; \"comparable or superior\" framing is misleading\n- k=75 is uncontrolled degree of freedom\n- Ablation robustness only on IOI (6x variation in CSS advantage)\n\n\n- Different method sets confound cross-model comparison\n\nStrengths: ACDC binarization pathology, honest framing, including negative results\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5**\nKey issues:\n- CSS is mathematically identical to feature selection stability index (unacknowledged)\n- Only 14 references, severely underreferenced\n- No head-to-head with Mondorf et al.'s continuous averaging\n- CIRCUS comparison uses potentially broken implementation\n- No size-controlled baselines\n- No confidence intervals on CPR differences\n\nStrengths: Binarization pathology finding, honest diagnostic framing, research question is timely\n\nNow I need to conduct the roundtable discussion to synthesize these perspectives.\n\nBoth reviewers converged on a score of 3 with high confidence, and they both identified the ACDC binarization pathology as the paper's strongest contribution while appreciating the honest diagnostic framing. The key overlaps also include concerns about the n=5 statistical limitation and incomplete ablation robustness, though they diverge on some technical details like the CSS novelty question and reference coverage.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8941,"cache_read_input_